  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Web Apps",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Welcome to Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how web apps shape daily life by building and testing a paper prototype. Through hands-on design and peer testing, they discover common structural components of web apps and recognize that layout decisions are intentional, not random. Students connect these patterns to how web apps guide user behavior and interaction.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Paper App Prototype Activity Guides (1 per student)\n- Print and cut mystery component sets (1 per pair)\n- Print copies of the Unit Guides (1 per student)\n- Provide tape or glue (1 per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nIn this lesson, you design and test your own paper web app. You discover that web apps follow shared design patterns and that their structure shapes how people interact online."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Web Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
      "name": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why do some web apps feel easy and others frustrating?**\n\nStudents explore how technology is designed for people. They compare old and modern websites, uncover the concept of Human-Centered Design (HCD), and connect it to the real-world work of User Experience (UX) Designers. Through a guided critique activity, students practice empathy by analyzing how different users experience different web apps and suggest improvements that make the design more inclusive.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the The User’s Experience (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**Why do some web apps feel easy and others frustrating?**\n\nIn this lesson, you’ll explore how technology is designed for people. You’ll compare old and modern websites to see how design has changed, learn about Human-Centered Design (HCD), and discover what User Experience (UX) Designers do. Then, you’ll practice empathy by looking at how different users experience different web apps and suggest ways to make the design easier and more inclusive."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nIn this lesson, students use AI as a design partner to build their first simple web page. They explore how AI generates code, practice crafting and refining prompts, and apply version control to save and revert their work. Through hands-on iteration, students see how AI can speed up creation while humans remain responsible for reviewing, improving, and shaping the final design.",
        "preparation": "- Print and cut out copies of the Feature Roulette Cards (one set of cards for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nIn this lesson, you use AI to help design and build a simple web page. By experimenting with prompts and saving different versions, you explore how AI and humans work together to create and improve designs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we draw inspiration from existing designs to guide our own?**\n\nIn this lesson, students analyze how visual style communicates mood and audience fit. They examine strengths and weaknesses in existing designs, then apply what they notice by creating AI-assisted mood boards that express a clear aesthetic direction. Through reflection and peer critique, students practice giving constructive, professional-style design feedback.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Style Critique activity guide (one for each student)\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can we draw inspiration from existing designs to guide our own?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore how different design styles communicate mood and audience. You create a mood board that expresses your own visual direction and give feedback to help others strengthen their designs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do fonts and colors tell a story?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of marketing specialists to explore how color, typography, and tone communicate brand identity. They analyze real-world brands to uncover design patterns and audience targeting, then design their own accessible, inclusive brand kits. Through peer feedback and reflection, students connect ethical and aesthetic decision-making, recognizing how design and AI can shape perception, representation, and inclusivity.",
        "student_overview": "**How do fonts and colors tell a story?**\n\nIn this lesson, you act as a marketing specialist to explore how colors, fonts, and style choices communicate a brand’s identity. You create a mini brand kit that reflects your audience’s needs while keeping your design accessible, inclusive, and intentional."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we make our web app reflect our brand identity?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of front-end web designers, translating visual brand identity into working code through CSS. They connect design and function by generating, applying, and refining AI-assisted CSS to achieve consistency across webpages. Through guided debugging and validation, students learn that professional coding practice involves reviewing AI outputs for accuracy, accessibility, and alignment with design intent.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we make our web app reflect our brand identity?**\n\nIn this lesson, you act as a front-end web designer, using CSS to turn brand designs into working websites. You apply and refine AI-generated styles to make pages consistent, accessible, and aligned with a clear visual identity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we define our user’s needs clearly?**\n\nStudents learn a process for analyzing user personas in order to identify needs and constraints. These observations translate into design implications and decisions. As a result, students finally draft problem statements that directly inform application design.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we define our user’s needs clearly?**\n\nAnalyze a user persona to spot real needs and limits, turn those insights into smart design choices, and write a clear problem statement that will steer your app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we break down a site into parts?**\n\nThis lesson positions students as UX Designers who create and test site maps to plan how users move through a website. Through iterative design and AI collaboration, students translate their structures into clear, precise prompts that guide an AI to build connected web pages. The work emphasizes clarity of logic, user-centered thinking, and effective communication with AI to ensure design intent is accurately reflected in the output.",
        "preparation": "- Print and cut out copies of the Website Cards for the Warm Up activity (one set of cards for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**How do we break down a site into parts?**\n\nIn this lesson, you take on the role of a UX Designer to plan how users move through a website and guide an AI to build it. The focus is on creating clear, logical structures and communicating your design ideas so the AI understands your intent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how accessible design ensures that digital experiences work for everyone, regardless of ability. They analyze real-world accessibility challenges and AI-generated designs to understand how bias and inaccessibility can be replicated through training data. By applying the POUR framework, students practice identifying accessibility issues and writing AI prompts that make designs more inclusive—reinforcing empathy, user-centered thinking, and the importance of human oversight in guiding AI.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Web Page Features activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nIn this lesson, you explore how to design websites and apps that work for everyone. By examining common accessibility issues and using the POUR framework, you learn how designers and AI can create more inclusive digital experiences when guided by human empathy and clear design choices."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of UX Designers to explore how sketches and wireframes help plan structure and user flow before any visual design or AI tools come into play. Through rapid sketching, analysis, and peer feedback, they learn that wireframing emphasizes clarity, consistency, and human decision-making in the design process. The lesson reinforces how designers use abstraction and pattern recognition to stay in control of structure and logic before automation or coding begins.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the Wireframe activity guide if your students will not use Sketch Lab on Level 1",
        "student_overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nIn this lesson, you act as a UX Designer to plan how a website or app is organized before any visuals or code are added. By sketching and refining wireframes, you focus on structure, clarity, and the design choices that belong to humans before AI takes over."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "name": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nStudents bring their wireframes to life by guiding AI to generate their first web layouts. They compare what AI builds to their original plan, then refine and debug to make each version clearer, more accurate, and more accessible.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nYou’ll turn your wireframe into a real web layout using AI. Then you’ll test, refine, and fix it so your design looks and works the way you planned."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
      "name": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How should layouts adapt across devices?**\n\nStudents examine how websites adapt across devices to understand responsiveness and test AI-generated layouts for usability issues. They take on the role of Software Test Engineers to identify and fix layout problems, connecting responsive design to accessibility and inclusion.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Designing for Responsiveness activity guide (one for each student).",
        "student_overview": "**How should layouts adapt across devices?**\n\nTest and improve your AI-generated webpage like a Software Test Engineer, by finding and fixing layout issues to make your design more responsive."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
      "name": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we bring our design plan to life in a way that meets our user’s needs?**\n\nIn this three-day project, students apply their design, accessibility, and AI skills to create a multi-page digital magazine that reflects a theme they care about. Working through structured milestones, they plan their site’s concept and audience, wireframe its structure, build and refine pages with consistent branding, and test for accessibility using the POUR framework. The project emphasizes human-in-the-loop design, iteration with AI, and reflection on how technology can enhance—but not replace—creative and inclusive design thinking.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Digital Magazine Project Planning Guide to students (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we bring our design plan to life in a way that meets our user’s needs?**\n\nIn this project, you design and build a digital magazine that reflects a theme or interest you care about. You plan your site’s structure, use AI to help create and refine your pages, and make sure your final design is consistent, accessible, and user-friendly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 15,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
