  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "8ec57063-d9db-43b8-beef-8053871447a4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ec57063-d9db-43b8-beef-8053871447a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cbdb20-258c-445c-a1b1-45a6968794c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4aa92abe-6e2f-479e-afe7-317589295b8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** The goal is to help students recognize that Web Apps are interactive systems defined by their structure and response, regardless of how they are accessed. It shifts their focus from \"software icons\" to the design patterns (like menus and buttons) that make those apps usable across different devices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the scenario on the slide aloud. Give students a moment to jot down their responses. Have students share with a partner and then call on a few volunteers to share with the class. As students respond, write key verbs on the board (e.g., message, buy, upload, search, post, edit).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students mentioning specific actions like \"ordering food,\" \"checking grades,\" or messaging friends\" rather than app names. If students get stuck, prompt with: \"Could you still search? Still message? Still buy something?\" ",
        "name": "The App-Less Day",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on the app itself, redirect with: \"What are you actually doing in that app?\" or \"What action are you trying to complete.\" "
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4aa92abe-6e2f-479e-afe7-317589295b8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b706a40d-79da-435e-90dc-54d87dcc0f3f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that even without installing an app, you still expect to message, search, post, or buy. That’s because what matters isn’t the icon, it’s the interaction.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b706a40d-79da-435e-90dc-54d87dcc0f3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d42b80f7-5d66-4545-890e-20cb242f331a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to define web apps. Emphasize the word respond and connect it to the verbs students just shared. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d42b80f7-5d66-4545-890e-20cb242f331a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7ca34b0-73c5-4623-9ac4-29b354078d34",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice, you didn’t say ‘open Instagram.’ You said things like message, search, and post. A web app isn’t just information. It responds to your actions. Today, we’re going to look at how those responses are intentionally designed and how web apps affect our lives.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7ca34b0-73c5-4623-9ac4-29b354078d34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bfb6797-5ac3-4a50-818c-86296cc6da89",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bfb6797-5ac3-4a50-818c-86296cc6da89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f000551-af23-461f-a83c-06a50b3d9b99",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students discover that web pages are intentionally structured and that visible parts work together to support user interaction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Paper App Prototype activity guide and one set of the mystery components.",
        "name": "Paper App Prototype",
        "progression_name": "Paper App Prototype Activity Guide",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For best results, provide printed copies so students can physically build and test their paper prototypes. If needed, students may use the digital activity guide linked in Level 1 instead."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f000551-af23-461f-a83c-06a50b3d9b99",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbf2ede8-a5d5-4969-bf08-5bc0f05ad14a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Web App Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbf2ede8-a5d5-4969-bf08-5bc0f05ad14a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c86b7686-8964-4b10-9502-007f531a3304",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the expectations for Steps 1 and 2. Clarify that students must choose one app purpose and arrange all four mystery components on the back of their activity guide to create a \"working\" app layout. Give students approximately 10 minutes to complete these two steps before moving on to Step 3. Remind students that their goal is to not make the app look good and perfect. Their goal is to make the app \"work\" for users. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, pay attention to how students are using the components to build their app and commonalities among placements. Listen for reasoning about user actions. Also encourage students to think through different actions a user might do in their app. If students focus only on visuals, redirect with: “What can the user actually do here?” or “What happens after they click that?” Encourage them to think in actions and responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, ask them to double-check that each component supports the app’s purpose and is placed intentionally.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c86b7686-8964-4b10-9502-007f531a3304",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6551a909-ba88-49e4-8db7-287861cbf413",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6551a909-ba88-49e4-8db7-287861cbf413",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60080836-5176-431f-acd1-617a56ff88ec",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will now “user test” each other’s paper apps. Pair each group with another pair. Instruct one group to act as users while the other acts as their app. Give them 4-5 minutes to test, then switch roles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for whether students are thinking in actions and responses. Listen for moments where a tester says, “What happens when I click this?” If an app has no clear response, prompt designers to add one. Encourage students to think in terms of: User action → App response.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If groups finish early, ask: “Did the app actually help you complete the goal?”, “Where did you get stuck?”, or “What did you expect to happen that didn’t?”\n\nKeep this step tight, spending 8-10 minutes total including the role switch.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60080836-5176-431f-acd1-617a56ff88ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff9f2297-c6d2-4926-b813-22e1fe39309e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff9f2297-c6d2-4926-b813-22e1fe39309e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25e26507-2ff9-48e5-bf81-d7eb51a9399e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will now compare their designs with two other groups. Have pairs first meet with a group that chose the same app and discuss the questions in their activity guide for 2-3 minutes. Then have them meet with a group that chose a different app and discuss the same questions. After about 5 minutes total, bring the class back together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, listen for students noticing similar component placement, especially elements placed at the top or grouped together. Pay attention to moments where a “user” taps something and the “app” doesn’t respond. If groups get stuck, prompt them by asking what surprised them, whether they noticed anything showing up in similar locations, or what happened when someone tried to use the layout.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is limited, prioritize the same-app comparison — that’s where pattern recognition is strongest and the “design is intentional” insight usually emerges.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The Paper App Prototype could be collected and used as a formative assessment of the learning objectives. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25e26507-2ff9-48e5-bf81-d7eb51a9399e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51573734-b6d1-4490-9341-99567fa3f66b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51573734-b6d1-4490-9341-99567fa3f66b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71b32b33-6cc1-4a5e-bbf6-dde0b8d7204a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- What did you notice about where most groups placed their components?\n- Why do you think so many layouts looked similar?\n- When testing, what happened if a “User” clicked something the “App” hadn't planned for?\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that their layouts were not random. Even without instructions, many groups placed components in similar locations and expected certain parts to respond when clicked. Students should begin to realize that web app structure follows shared design patterns and that interaction only works when those parts are intentionally planned.",
        "progression_name": "Producing HTML and CSS"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71b32b33-6cc1-4a5e-bbf6-dde0b8d7204a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47b11a86-3b23-4c99-87d8-51b51648017d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47b11a86-3b23-4c99-87d8-51b51648017d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a7f491d-ba1e-4873-97ad-91d3304346e8",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just discovered that these parts of a web app aren’t random. Now let’s put names to the components you’ve already been using so you can record them in your Unit Guide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a7f491d-ba1e-4873-97ad-91d3304346e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ece4f081-d0ff-4009-aa6d-6b7edaf0d6cb",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ece4f081-d0ff-4009-aa6d-6b7edaf0d6cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24730a0b-114d-437b-9055-5225eb60a32e",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24730a0b-114d-437b-9055-5225eb60a32e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9292d4aa-58d8-4155-973a-d86c9479bd1f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Many of you placed this strip across the top in very similar ways. No one told you to do that. That’s because you’ve already learned how web apps are structured just by using them. You’ve been thinking like designers — even if you didn’t know the technical terms yet.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9292d4aa-58d8-4155-973a-d86c9479bd1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fca0e9fd-bb22-4c1e-b408-2864492d6ba6",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the first component name: Navigation Bar. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Then transition directly to the definition slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define navigation bar. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the pace brisk. Students already used these components in their prototypes — this moment is about naming and sharpening their understanding, not introducing something entirely new.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fca0e9fd-bb22-4c1e-b408-2864492d6ba6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdadf88b-d6b1-44f8-899b-6ede933dc882",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Hero Section. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Then transition directly to the definition slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define hero section. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdadf88b-d6b1-44f8-899b-6ede933dc882",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "895b45c3-a1b3-4737-8b67-cfc305119ba1",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Gallery Cards. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Then transition directly to the definition slide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define gallery cards. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "895b45c3-a1b3-4737-8b67-cfc305119ba1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08c27455-3fe6-48aa-b98f-ece988f9475f",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the final component name: Footer. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Then transition directly to the definition slide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define footer. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: HTML"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08c27455-3fe6-48aa-b98f-ece988f9475f",
