      }
    },
    {
      "key": "94da7696-f8bc-43f9-98a4-4e20fa4eae6e",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: CSS"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "94da7696-f8bc-43f9-98a4-4e20fa4eae6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9020fd0e-91fc-422a-afc2-bf0a1bec01f6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them 2–3 minutes to write independently. Then invite a few volunteers to share. As students respond, press for connections to today’s activity (patterns, interaction, intentional placement).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that web apps shape our daily lives because they are interactive systems built around shared design patterns. These patterns allow us to quickly understand how to navigate, search, buy, post, and communicate. Students should understand that web apps are intentionally structured by designers to support specific actions and responses — and that this structure influences how we think, move, and make decisions online.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, reinforce these connections explicitly:\n- Web apps shape our lives because they respond to what we do — they aren’t just pages, they are systems.\n- We navigate quickly because designers use shared layout patterns (like navigation at the top and grouped content).\n- When structure changes, our experience changes.\n- Behind every web app is a team of humans making decisions about how it should look, feel, and function\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9020fd0e-91fc-422a-afc2-bf0a1bec01f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0faba28d-1142-45ee-bf21-0b6965c90bac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the \"What You'll Do in Unit 1\" slide to connect students’ prototype work and QOTD thinking to the focus of Unit 1. Emphasize that students made intentional layout decisions about structure and interaction, and clarify that Unit 1 strengthens design thinking before moving into coding in later units.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0faba28d-1142-45ee-bf21-0b6965c90bac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "639f8b7d-8fb1-4a1c-ba75-74e472a0ba10",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you made intentional layout decisions. That’s design. You didn’t need code to do it. This is what Unit 1 is about, learning to think and plan like designers before writing code.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide is an important bridge between today’s prototype activity and the rest of Unit 1. Take a moment to reinforce that design decisions, not coding, are the focus of this unit.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "639f8b7d-8fb1-4a1c-ba75-74e472a0ba10",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a9b8f3a-adaa-462d-8316-4578bb232a2a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a9b8f3a-adaa-462d-8316-4578bb232a2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ed74220-8a9d-4041-8fea-21c8a16b6382",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ed74220-8a9d-4041-8fea-21c8a16b6382",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5706f26d-3f92-4387-bf49-c932affbf79e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5706f26d-3f92-4387-bf49-c932affbf79e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "392afb0b-e753-4e7c-aff2-7f4b62304c83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with a visual, real-world example that makes usability differences obvious. Help them recognize that design evolves to meet user needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show two versions of the same website from different years (e.g., Amazon in 2000 and 2025)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "392afb0b-e753-4e7c-aff2-7f4b62304c83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1dc3e61b-52ec-45ee-84ea-9106f389e0c4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's take a look at how web design has changed over time. Here's what Amazon.com looked like in 2000 and 2025. Take a moment to look closely at the differences in design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1dc3e61b-52ec-45ee-84ea-9106f389e0c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24fce8b6-92e0-480f-b24a-7cb9cff76aa9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version would you rather use and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students noticing specific usability improvements, not just visual ones. Look for comments that highlight how modern websites feel easier to navigate, look cleaner and less cluttered, and are more accessible or efficient. Students might mention clearer menus, readable text, or AI-driven personalization that helps them find what they need faster.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on looks, prompt them to think about usability. Ask, \"What makes it easier to find what you need?\" or \"How might this help different users?\" Highlight features like search bars or cleaner layouts as signs of human-centered, accessible design. You can also note that modern sites adapt to users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24fce8b6-92e0-480f-b24a-7cb9cff76aa9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd7cee96-0fff-4923-aeb2-974dd844d3fe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd7cee96-0fff-4923-aeb2-974dd844d3fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a137372e-737b-4114-8edb-304af3e7915d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've compared the old and new Amazon sites, let's take a closer look at the newer one. It's a great example of how modern websites are designed around people's everyday needs — clearer visuals, faster access, and more personal experiences.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a137372e-737b-4114-8edb-304af3e7915d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5fa8006-9095-46fd-ab10-62e6b41129a6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\nConsider the web page features you've learned about:\n- Nav Bar\n- Hero Section\n- Cards\n- Footer (not visible here)\n\nHow do these features make the site easier to use?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify how common web page features such as the navigation bar, hero section, cards, and footer support user needs by making information easier to find, compare, and act on. They should recognize that these design choices reflect real user habits like scanning quickly, previewing items before deciding, and completing tasks efficiently.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention AI features such as \"it knows what I want,\" briefly affirm the idea and connect it to design choices that respond to user behavior. For example, you might say, \"Yes, some websites use tools that learn from what people click so they can make things easier to find.\" You can also point out that many modern sites adjust to different devices and screen sizes, which shows that designers think about how people actually use technology."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5fa8006-9095-46fd-ab10-62e6b41129a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31097402-e843-467d-9272-594a652058f8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31097402-e843-467d-9272-594a652058f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aaf796e0-592f-491f-98cf-2dd54d01cfb2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aaf796e0-592f-491f-98cf-2dd54d01cfb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "142d2369-4d96-4e4c-bf8d-38bed733d89b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Establish the foundational concepts of user-centered design, user profiles, and user experience (UX) to prepare students for critiquing digital experiences.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "User Experience"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "142d2369-4d96-4e4c-bf8d-38bed733d89b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c7ad3c1-660e-4414-9ac3-4e67c7eb31e5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll step into the role of a UX designer. You'll look at your app through a user's eyes and think about how design affects different people",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c7ad3c1-660e-4414-9ac3-4e67c7eb31e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6a8289a-d4d9-4ef9-aaa8-ba71629eb756",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to review the three UX Designer tasks: Observe, Evaluate, Suggest. Explain that these are the same steps real designers use to make technology more human-centered.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6a8289a-d4d9-4ef9-aaa8-ba71629eb756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0005d12a-a3ef-481b-9f6f-042dfe960d62",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user profile."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0005d12a-a3ef-481b-9f6f-042dfe960d62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a373e1b-837f-4ab9-975c-253fe1c0c1f2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about what we just saw on the modern Amazon site: it's full of visuals, easier to navigate, and sometimes even changes based on the user. Those choices came from understanding what people need and how they actually shop. That's what it means to design with the user in mind. Now connect that idea to your user profile: which of these design decisions would matter most for Jordan, Mark, or Sam?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a373e1b-837f-4ab9-975c-253fe1c0c1f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "594acdbc-2d50-4751-b26f-bae1c436eed3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Amazon screenshot with callouts showing examples of human-centered design choices (e.g., large images, clear categories, personalized recommendations). Click through the animated slide to walk through each design decision one at a time. Then have students do a quick turn-and-talk: Which decision would help your user the most, and why?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "594acdbc-2d50-4751-b26f-bae1c436eed3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5655b73e-9def-4444-9f6c-15b784480c1e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've been thinking like designers already – noticing what works for users and what doesn't. Let's see how professionals do this in the real world.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5655b73e-9def-4444-9f6c-15b784480c1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bd60557-6e9b-414f-8a95-7a611d9fa841",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the clip from the \"What is UX Design?\" video (0:05-0:48)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bd60557-6e9b-414f-8a95-7a611d9fa841",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6da1cfd2-0e05-4f6f-916d-4c3161f4d125",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "User Experience Designers take all of these things into account when designing a webpage by practicing something called human-centered design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6da1cfd2-0e05-4f6f-916d-4c3161f4d125",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c115197-28ec-42d8-85c2-9d999eafc3f3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user experience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define human-centered design.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c115197-28ec-42d8-85c2-9d999eafc3f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cf654ed-cc02-4b14-b68e-116d524c652f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students practice empathy by seeing apps through the eyes of different users. Reinforce that usability depends on the context and the person, not just the design itself. Provide a structured, hands-on critique activity that makes user-centered design concrete.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of The User's Experience activity guide.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "See It Like a User"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cf654ed-cc02-4b14-b68e-116d524c652f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67dd7b1f-4db6-4802-836b-1cf7a48af005",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each person in your group will represent one user profile: Jordan, Mark, or Sam. Read your assigned profile carefully and think about that person's needs and frustrations. Consider what a well-designed page would look like for them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67dd7b1f-4db6-4802-836b-1cf7a48af005",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48ac358c-6b1f-4acc-8938-763d66f8821a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each student select a profile and annotate key needs in the activity guide before reviewing the app.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48ac358c-6b1f-4acc-8938-763d66f8821a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a8454ef-ec8c-40c3-bbda-75b1e1430f44",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1. Students should complete the task as their selected user, then complete the Usability Checklist in their activity guide based on that experience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students evaluating the app from their user's perspective and supporting their checklist responses with specific details from the profile.\n",
        "progression_name": "Usability Checklist",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students move through the checklist quickly without explanation, prompt them to connect each decision to the user profile. Ask, \"What in the profile supports that?\" Encourage user-specific reasoning such as, \"Mark might struggle with this small text because of vision changes,\" rather than general comments like, \"The text is small.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a8454ef-ec8c-40c3-bbda-75b1e1430f44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d6a28eb-5e27-4503-b71a-4edd73972a0a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 2. Have them adjust the app using the control panel to better support their selected user. Remind them they may change up to three settings and must document their reasoning in the activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students making purposeful changes that connect clearly to their user's needs. Check that they are completing the rationale, user impact, and tradeoff for each adjustment.",
        "progression_name": "App Redesign",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus only on \"making it look better,\" ask, \"How does this change specifically help your user?\" Encourage them to consider tradeoffs and think about how a change that helps one user might create challenges for another."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d6a28eb-5e27-4503-b71a-4edd73972a0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a189b31-0f33-42f8-a644-05a154a1ed53",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students clearly explaining both parts of the question: why the Level 1 app lacked usability and clarity for their user, and how their redesign applied user-centered design. If students are vague, ask, \"How did that specific change improve usability?\" or \"What part of the user profile supports your reasoning?\"",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this prompt as a formative assessment to determine whether students can critique usability and clarity and explain how their redesign reflects user-centered design."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to move beyond general statements like \"it was hard to use\" and focus on clarity and usability. Prompt them to explain how their change intentionally addressed a specific user need."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a189b31-0f33-42f8-a644-05a154a1ed53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0fa19cd3-37cf-4637-918b-a71afe53c060",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Consolidate the lesson by naming Human-Centered Design explicitly. Transition students from activity-based observations to formal vocabulary and concepts. Provide an early writing artifact that demonstrates growth in HCD understanding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0fa19cd3-37cf-4637-918b-a71afe53c060",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a100f41-4f97-4b25-bf9f-d0576a334ce2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We've spent today looking at how different people use apps and websites. We've seen how design choices can make things easy or frustrating. This whole process is part of something called Human-Centered Design, or HCD. It means that good design always starts with empathy – truly understanding and caring about the users.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a100f41-4f97-4b25-bf9f-d0576a334ce2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f506085-21dc-45e3-b3f4-662a146397cb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to share what they wrote for the Level 3 prompt: \"The Level 1 app felt difficult for [user’s name] because _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ I applied user-centered design by changing _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _, which improved usability and clarity by _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _.\" Encourage them to refer back to their activity guide to reference their usability checklist and app redesign table. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f506085-21dc-45e3-b3f4-662a146397cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b782fb7-5fe8-444c-bfb0-e568abcafd13",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that AI can generate web layouts quickly using pattern recognition, but it still requires human oversight to ensure quality, accuracy, and usability.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b782fb7-5fe8-444c-bfb0-e568abcafd13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9c0e168-c49c-43cf-beb4-7daec68271a3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9c0e168-c49c-43cf-beb4-7daec68271a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76a1804d-fe13-4c1c-9086-4185d687e4de",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** What are all the things generative AI can do for you? List as many as you can think of.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Right now students may think that AI is just ChatGPT or image generation. They may have used AI to help them write essays or act as a tutor, but they don’t realize that they can use AI to build out full apps. Help students understand that modern AI tools can generate text, images, code, designs, and even entire working web pages. That said, AI should not be used to replace developers but as a tool to make developers’ work more efficient."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76a1804d-fe13-4c1c-9086-4185d687e4de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fef436d-0cda-4b29-aa2b-9a5309c6061f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fef436d-0cda-4b29-aa2b-9a5309c6061f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3b87389-f293-4cbf-b6c4-df766ccb2baa",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we look at what the AI actually generates, let’s pause and make a prediction. Take a moment to read the prompt carefully and think about the restaurant websites you’ve seen before. If you were asked to design this website, what would you create? What sections, design elements, or buttons usually show up on sites like this? Think about the patterns you’ve noticed across similar websites.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3b87389-f293-4cbf-b6c4-df766ccb2baa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbfe0de5-ef1d-465f-bc4c-d2df4d81d944",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbfe0de5-ef1d-465f-bc4c-d2df4d81d944",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1aa6de63-1d7f-4b6c-bac2-0350de0b033c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What sections will likely appear?*\n- *What elements do you expect?*\n- *What words will show up?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Likely, students will draw from their own experience and different restaurant websites they have seen in order to create a gourmet pizza restaurant. Students should understand that AI works the same way. AI will generate a predictable layout because it follows common design and language patterns.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1aa6de63-1d7f-4b6c-bac2-0350de0b033c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62cb0bd2-7e83-4962-9627-47d95d63a648",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62cb0bd2-7e83-4962-9627-47d95d63a648",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "378d8505-2425-4d13-9a7a-e87c47a70c14",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What matched your predictions?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect what they predicted to what actually appeared. Students analyze the AI-generated webpage and identify how it reflects common website conventions, such as having a nav bar at the top of the page with familiar buttons. They should recognize that the layout, language, and structure feel familiar because AI follows widely used patterns, not because it “understands” restaurants.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "378d8505-2425-4d13-9a7a-e87c47a70c14",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02bf17c8-0fb5-4460-b954-b868b99220cf",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02bf17c8-0fb5-4460-b954-b868b99220cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc014145-95de-4884-bcf0-0573aec7c522",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Ask students whether they would publish the AI-generated address as is or edit it before making the site live. Have them explain their reasoning as if they were the developer responsible for the website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that AI-generated content may look realistic but can contain errors or fabricated details. They understand that developers are responsible for reviewing, verifying, and refining AI output before publishing. The goal is to reinforce that AI is a tool that supports development, but humans remain accountable for accuracy, functionality, and safety.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say they would trust the AI without checking, guide them to think about potential risks. Ask what could happen if the address were incorrect or if a small mistake went unnoticed. Prompt them to consider who would be responsible if customers showed up at the wrong location or if the site malfunctioned. Use this moment to reinforce that responsibility belongs to the human developer, not the AI.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc014145-95de-4884-bcf0-0573aec7c522",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96dd7fa6-ee79-4294-a269-93f2fdd959da",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96dd7fa6-ee79-4294-a269-93f2fdd959da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f6124c7-3b0f-455c-bee5-d57441e60237",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f6124c7-3b0f-455c-bee5-d57441e60237",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7f71c5a-d6fa-4d10-b0d1-4650a31b5d6d",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7f71c5a-d6fa-4d10-b0d1-4650a31b5d6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89bcaf28-f0e8-4171-ba35-cce05956a976",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how generative AI uses training data and pattern recognition to make predictions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the slide to explain to students that before they start using AI to write code, they should understand how AI “knows” what to generate. Remind students that AI doesn't really understand code the way a developer does. Instead, AI predicts and recreates patterns it has seen in examples of real web pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students’ attention to the collection of website examples feeding into the brain graphic. Explain that these represent the training data the AI learned from before anyone ever typed a prompt. Clarify that training data consists of real websites created by humans and that the AI studied many examples to learn from them. Emphasize that the AI does not begin with built-in knowledge about restaurants or web design, it learns from large collections of human-created content. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the repeated website elements shown on the slide and explain that the AI looks across many examples to identify what appears frequently. Highlight specific repeated features such as navigation bars at the top, red call-to-action buttons, large headlines, and menu sections with prices. Introduce the term “patterns” as structures or design choices that show up again and again across many sites. Reinforce that this step is about noticing similarities, not generating anything yet. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students’ attention to the brain-to-website diagram and explain that this step represents generation. Clarify that after identifying patterns, the AI produces new content by predicting what usually comes next based on what it has learned. Emphasize that the AI is not copying a single website but creating something new that fits the patterns it observed. Connect this explanation back to the pizza webpage students analyzed earlier to show how the prediction step results in the final output. \n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "How AI Makes Predictions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89bcaf28-f0e8-4171-ba35-cce05956a976",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89a80367-e96a-436b-829e-c605b0479ddb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize how to prompt AI for structure and style of their webpages using a requirements focused lens – stating the goals, constraints, and edge cases.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present HTML and CSS and ask students what the difference is between these two images. Explain that HTML provides the structure, text, and content of a page, while CSS adds the style and visual design. Direct students to look closely at the two versions of the page on the slide. Have them record in their activity guide what they think HTML is controlling and what CSS is controlling based on what they notice in each image.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Prompting for HTML and CSS ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to share a few observations before you confirm or elaborate. Use simple analogies to keep it intuitive:\n- HTML is the skeleton or blueprint that shapes the page.\n- CSS is the paint, layout, and personality that make it visually appealing.\n\nRemind students that they don't need to know any code yet—just focus on what seems to belong to structure versus style.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89a80367-e96a-436b-829e-c605b0479ddb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58afeb59-62e1-4526-a00e-81810b147b47",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58afeb59-62e1-4526-a00e-81810b147b47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4fae0563-dbdc-4e7e-890b-90a9ff00336a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this unit, you'll act as a web developer to plan and build web pages with AI assistance. The first step to using generative AI effectively, is knowing how to prompt it. ",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4fae0563-dbdc-4e7e-890b-90a9ff00336a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a18f2a99-34d9-4ad2-90d0-7ff86d441d78",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly review the three requirements that students need to include when writing AI prompts. Explain that they'll get more detailed guidance and examples in the following slides.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a18f2a99-34d9-4ad2-90d0-7ff86d441d78",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad20421f-8d05-4eca-b021-c4a62c177418",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad20421f-8d05-4eca-b021-c4a62c177418",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0db34686-f33b-4d5b-b62a-6670db56b209",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which AI prompt is better? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it gives a specific HTML layout for AI to generate. They should recognize that while Prompt #1 describes the same type of web app, it is too vague and open-ended. Likely, Prompt #1 will lead AI to generate something different from what the student actually wanted. Help students identify the difference between a vague goal (\"a menu app\") in Prompt #1 and a specific goal (\"3x4 grid,\" \"price in bold,\" \"bulleted list of ingredients\") in Prompt #2.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The more specific students can get in drafting their prompt the less back and forth they will have to do with AI later in the development process.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0db34686-f33b-4d5b-b62a-6670db56b209",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d47915c7-ebf3-4c11-b008-2442972e0b8d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d47915c7-ebf3-4c11-b008-2442972e0b8d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a8dc6cd2-7130-4ac9-99ed-d998adcb617f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which AI prompt is better? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it provides concrete, objective constraints like \"Dark Mode\" and \"sans-serif fonts\". They should recognize that \"nice\" and \"easy to read\" in Prompt #1 are subjective opinions that AI may interpret differently than the student.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Which AI prompt is better? Why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #1 is better because it provides specific, logical behaviors for the AI to program into the app, like \"zero inventory.\" They should recognize that while Prompt #2 identifies potential problems like \"weird orders\" or \"no internet,\" it doesn't give the AI any instructions on how to handle them.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a8dc6cd2-7130-4ac9-99ed-d998adcb617f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c223f16e-aff1-469c-9d12-1121cd368953",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c223f16e-aff1-469c-9d12-1121cd368953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1899a295-ae57-4acc-851a-6a896f4298d9",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prompt engineering*. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1899a295-ae57-4acc-851a-6a896f4298d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b12435b-3b71-447a-a1e9-95202b68370a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b12435b-3b71-447a-a1e9-95202b68370a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df2abb80-4d28-43a6-9721-13f4bfe1ae5a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice prompting AI for HTML and CSS by stating the goals, constraints, and edge cases required for their user persona’s web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- **Level 1**: Students practice the ROPE prompting framework and draft their own prompt.\n- **Level 2**: Students review how to prompt AI using the Accept/Reject flow and save their work.\n- **Level 3**: Students prompt AI to create the HTML for their webpage.\n- **Level 4**: Students prompt AI to create the CSS for their webpage.\n- **Level 5**: Students review how to roll back version history.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, check that they're using details from their prompts (not just \"make a nav bar\"). Ask students questions like, \"What constraints did you give to AI?\" or \"How well did AI do in matching your expected output?\" Make sure to reinforce that iteration—trying, refining, and saving—is how developers manage complex builds.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Prompting with AI",
        "progression_name": "Plan Your AI Prompt",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that refining prompts and prompting AI is normal and expected. Developers don't just write perfect code once. They iterate, version, and improve, just like you.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df2abb80-4d28-43a6-9721-13f4bfe1ae5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f153896-fb66-48c3-a3f2-125542c04821",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f153896-fb66-48c3-a3f2-125542c04821",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14b90b2a-8095-48cf-bcc5-849ea8d2a142",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *versioning*. Direct students to fill in the vocab definition in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "progression_name": "Web Page Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14b90b2a-8095-48cf-bcc5-849ea8d2a142",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62537cfc-35be-494d-b584-277ed3b6a919",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62537cfc-35be-494d-b584-277ed3b6a919",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "378ce071-67ad-40dc-b749-070aff7d8a9c",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy set of the Feature Roulette Cards.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Building with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "378ce071-67ad-40dc-b749-070aff7d8a9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3922f3d4-170b-4288-b158-4f8a08e4eb64",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Revert to a Previous Version"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3922f3d4-170b-4288-b158-4f8a08e4eb64",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f857318-f2cd-47d0-adbd-c8d194391f2f",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 6. Explain that this is their chance to apply everything they've learned to finish building their first AI-assisted web page. Tell students they'll draw one card at a time from each category – HTML, CSS, Version History, and Try it Out – and follow the instructions. Encourage students to refer back to the panel levels (Level 2 and 5) as needed. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check if students are successfully refining their prompts and if they understand how to revert to a previous version. Ask questions like: \"What did you change in your latest version?\" or \"How are you deciding when your page is finished?\" or \"What did the AI get right, or wrong, this time?\" Encourage students to compare different AI outputs and think critically about what each version improved or broke.\n",
        "progression_name": "AI Prompt Roulette",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that this activity is about experimentation and iteration. Developers rarely get a design perfect on the first try—they refine prompts, fix errors, and test new versions to get closer to their vision. Connect this to professional practice: \"Developers use AI as a creative partner, but they're still responsible for making decisions and improving the final product.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f857318-f2cd-47d0-adbd-c8d194391f2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16535a44-fd20-4cf7-9b95-72c1c827f299",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student interactions and final AI-generated pages to gauge understanding of how to apply prompting strategies, refine results, and using version history. Listen for language that shows students can explain their design choices or how they fixed an issue."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16535a44-fd20-4cf7-9b95-72c1c827f299",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efbf6c24-0596-4de5-8a7a-4a01f3a33df1",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efbf6c24-0596-4de5-8a7a-4a01f3a33df1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9eb31b8-e2fe-4270-9b2b-65d2938a0d2f",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why might AI sometimes misunderstand what we mean?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students hypothesize possible causes of AI misunderstanding prompts—such as unclear wording, missing details, conflicting instructions, or biases in training data. The goal isn't for them to know exactly how models work, but to start reasoning about why clarity and iteration are essential in coding with AI.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Validate all ideas. Emphasize that AI misunderstandings are normal and highlight why iteration matters: developers test, adjust, and refine prompts to guide AI toward what they want.\nIf discussion stalls, prompt students with:\n- Have you ever written a message and someone misunderstood you? What caused that?\n- How could that happen between a person and an AI?\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9eb31b8-e2fe-4270-9b2b-65d2938a0d2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "788feda8-40d2-46aa-baff-6886154111b1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to draw on what they noticed while prompting the AI, specifically how it generated HTML and CSS from prompts but often needed human refinement or direction. After a minute, invite a few volunteers to share highlights with the class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the discussion to reinforce the big takeaway: AI can accelerate creation, but developers still guide, test, and refine what it produces. You might ask:\n- What did the AI help you build today?\n- When did you provide your own ideas to help AI make a decision?\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "788feda8-40d2-46aa-baff-6886154111b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b6e9a1ed-0ca7-49d9-b64c-c996d5c72b96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7d9c976-94b2-49e2-96cf-8d4248226efa",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7d9c976-94b2-49e2-96cf-8d4248226efa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b6e9a1ed-0ca7-49d9-b64c-c996d5c72b96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd71d3f7-0fa9-41c3-b652-ac3122ad11b9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd71d3f7-0fa9-41c3-b652-ac3122ad11b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b6e9a1ed-0ca7-49d9-b64c-c996d5c72b96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0112596-fbb4-4b04-9729-5a541c8cd420",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the first of two websites that varies in style (e.g., clean, cluttered, playful, serious). Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Encourage quick, instinctive responses—students shouldn't overthink.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students hesitate, prompt with: “What’s the very first word that pops into your head?” Reassure them there are no wrong answers—every impression is valid. Keep this quick and instinctive.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0112596-fbb4-4b04-9729-5a541c8cd420",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "658d95d2-4279-4d75-af28-f4cbf905a078",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "658d95d2-4279-4d75-af28-f4cbf905a078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "217b3b22-a8f4-44e9-95af-dff98db06087",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the second website, which has an elegant, high-contrast design. Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Continue to encourage quick, instinctive responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the pace brisk. This is about noticing differences in tone and style—not analyzing them yet.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "217b3b22-a8f4-44e9-95af-dff98db06087",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "164c3405-1315-4de0-92ea-4d2f68185a84",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "164c3405-1315-4de0-92ea-4d2f68185a84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1cc59677-fcb0-41b6-8ef3-e2239152c47d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Gather student responses for each site (use the board, sticky notes, or a digital tool). As students share their first-impression words and ideas for who each site might be for, record patterns such as repeated words, shared audiences, or clear differences between designs.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only share one-word answers without elaboration, ask a quick follow-up like:\n- \"What about that site made you think it was for that audience?\"\n- \"What design choice made you think of that word?\"\n- \"Do you think most users would see it that way, or was that your personal take?\"\n\nThis keeps the activity brisk but adds depth, helping students connect instinctive impressions and audience guesses to concrete design elements (e.g., color, layout, font, imagery)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1cc59677-fcb0-41b6-8ef3-e2239152c47d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc34b66a-e9aa-4baf-87e5-b2f3a462173f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc34b66a-e9aa-4baf-87e5-b2f3a462173f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f169f3dc-407c-42dc-99fc-387c6f72f4bd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice how some of your words and audience ideas overlap while others are totally different. That shows how quickly design choices communicate — and how much style affects who a site appeals to.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f169f3dc-407c-42dc-99fc-387c6f72f4bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17de11b2-3169-46f9-b2f4-c4afca9d8f92",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17de11b2-3169-46f9-b2f4-c4afca9d8f92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66170931-9e27-4aa5-aa89-af34fbb25305",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze website design choices to identify strengths and weaknesses, using criteria like clarity, cohesion, and audience fit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Style Critique activity guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Style Critique",
        "progression_name": "Style Critique Activity Guide"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66170931-9e27-4aa5-aa89-af34fbb25305",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f35a4844-c4f6-4098-8805-1885b5b63f98",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f35a4844-c4f6-4098-8805-1885b5b63f98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27a0989b-8e9e-49b0-85a6-0ce0d76d818c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students annotate the website images in their Style Critique activity guide by labeling Strengths and Weaknesses. Remind them to think about clarity (is it easy to read?), cohesion (do the design elements feel consistent?), and audience fit (does the style match who the site is for?). Have students refer back to their Usability Checklist from Lesson 2 in their Unit Guides if needed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they're explaining their annotations. If students write vague notes (e.g., \"bad colors\"), prompt them to be specific (e.g., \"low contrast between text and background makes it hard to read\").",
        "progression_name": "Style Critique Activity Guide",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Model one example first — circle a feature and label it Strength or Weakness with a short note like \"easy to read\" or \"too crowded\". This shows students that their annotations don't need to be elaborate to be effective. \n\nFor advanced students, challenge them to explain how a weakness could be redesigned.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27a0989b-8e9e-49b0-85a6-0ce0d76d818c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "338b9743-57b4-40c5-a481-6b8a0b20a73f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "338b9743-57b4-40c5-a481-6b8a0b20a73f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ebc80107-20eb-4b64-a9f6-7d396c3e9397",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What made one site's design stronger than another?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify specific, observable design strengths and weaknesses (e.g., readability, layout consistency, color balance) and explain how those choices affect clarity and audience fit. Use this comparison to introduce the idea of competitive analysis—learning by examining what works and what doesn’t in different designs—and to establish criteria students will apply to their own mood boards.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students respond with vague responses (“It just looks better”), push for specificity and prompt with focused comparisons:\n\n- \"Which site was easier to read, and why?\"\n- \"Which site felt more consistent in style?\"\n- \"Which site seemed to fit its audience best?\"\n\nThis keeps the discussion brief and grounded in observable design choices rather than personal preference."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ebc80107-20eb-4b64-a9f6-7d396c3e9397",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f4dbc25a-f539-440e-92a2-d6eb0de46178",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f4dbc25a-f539-440e-92a2-d6eb0de46178",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1d1ea21-f1ba-433d-90b8-0b6e30884847",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice planning like designers by using a mood board to test visual direction before building.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Mood Boarding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1d1ea21-f1ba-433d-90b8-0b6e30884847",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df91e6d8-7818-4a1e-9c9a-fe89997d265c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df91e6d8-7818-4a1e-9c9a-fe89997d265c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf788a29-5c89-4109-8701-c224f3e75c1f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just analyzed finished designs. But designers don’t start with finished products, they start with exploring ideas. ",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf788a29-5c89-4109-8701-c224f3e75c1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3298b70f-9311-4404-a6e2-6186028d05fb",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3298b70f-9311-4404-a6e2-6186028d05fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd57988a-4bbe-4755-98e1-c73d8be6b5b7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Define mood boarding and direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide. Explain that designers use mood boards as a tool to collect visual inspiration. Emphasize that mood boards are a familiar way to express feeling or style, not to create a finished product.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect mood boards to contexts students know—like Pinterest boards, vision boards, or even outfit collages. This helps students see mood boards as familiar tools for expressing style choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd57988a-4bbe-4755-98e1-c73d8be6b5b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19a63aa9-b24f-41b2-bcf7-14a8a7ca4d1e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19a63aa9-b24f-41b2-bcf7-14a8a7ca4d1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31a84432-b0cf-46d3-9e42-58153ed283ef",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students briefly observe the three mood board examples and identify one thing they have in common. Take 2–3 quick responses and highlight shared patterns like color palette, tone, or consistency. Emphasize that designers use mood boards to explore and test ideas before building. This makes design intentional—not guesswork.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect back to the Style Critique: planning with a mood board helps prevent the weak or inconsistent design choices students just identified.\n\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31a84432-b0cf-46d3-9e42-58153ed283ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "597c0462-8d25-4476-b13b-c747eef553e9",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "597c0462-8d25-4476-b13b-c747eef553e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b527b5c7-c948-45eb-9047-458fb2f85183",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you'll work like visual designers—exploring style and mood before deciding on details.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b527b5c7-c948-45eb-9047-458fb2f85183",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff5e3567-d076-4b46-a083-602f0bcffceb",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff5e3567-d076-4b46-a083-602f0bcffceb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46b5d44c-d36e-4d90-87ee-ef5405ff30a6",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2-4.\n- Level 2a: Students use Sketch Lab to build a mood board that communicates a clear style.\n- Level 2b (Optional): Students may use the AI Chat Design Partner if they need help brainstorming colors, fonts, imagery, or style words before building.\n- Level 3: Students learn how to save to their “backpack” in Sketch Lab.\n- Level 4: Students refine their mood board and save it to their backpack.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they include all key components (colors, fonts/typography, images, and 2–3 style words). If students seem stuck or unsure how to begin, suggest they briefly visit the AI Chat Design Partner to generate ideas, then return to Sketch Lab to apply them intentionally. Encourage students who are only picking random images/colors to think about how their choices connect to the user experience and the vibe they want to communicate. Ask probing questions like: \"What feeling do you want your user to have when they see this?\" or \"Do these colors and fonts work together as a set?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Mood Board Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46b5d44c-d36e-4d90-87ee-ef5405ff30a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "666b4c67-273a-4226-9489-2b3a6887e2ff",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Mood Boarding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that mood boards don't need to be perfect or polished—they're meant to explore style direction. Suggest that students step back and ask, \"If someone else saw this board, would they instantly get the vibe I'm going for?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students can apply design critique concepts to their own work. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Choosing visuals that communicate a consistent mood or theme (clarity and cohesion).\n- Making design choices that fit a user or purpose (audience fit).\n- Using vocabulary like \"color palette,\" \"contrast,\" or \"style\" when explaining their decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "666b4c67-273a-4226-9489-2b3a6887e2ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1264cad-c2b7-4001-9456-6eb07f09d44f",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that professional designers share work before it’s finished. Emphasize that feedback helps them test whether their message is clear and whether the design works for its intended audience. Connect this back to students’ mood boards—they are still drafts, not final products.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that good feedback helps someone improve their design — it points to a specific element and explains why it works or what could be stronger. Briefly model the difference between helpful and vague feedback using the examples on the slide. Emphasize that helpful feedback focuses on the design choices, not personal preference."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1264cad-c2b7-4001-9456-6eb07f09d44f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6f31f55-9758-4e7e-8e9c-140e3d87460d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6f31f55-9758-4e7e-8e9c-140e3d87460d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66f24e0b-15aa-48be-b71c-dc53a306fcf7",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around and review mood boards created for the same use case. Each student leaves at least one sticky note using one of the sentence frames. Remind students this is practice giving specific, focused, actionable feedback to improve a design — not judging whether they personally like it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Circulate and listen for vague praise (“Looks good”). Pause and push for specificity by asking:\n- “What exactly communicates the style?”\n- “What design choice are you referring to?”\n- “What could they change to improve clarity or audience fit?”\nEncourage feedback that names a specific element (color, font, layout, imagery).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66f24e0b-15aa-48be-b71c-dc53a306fcf7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90c3c012-db85-457a-b2c0-ece76d31a94d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide and give them about two minutes to write independently. Then invite two or three volunteers to share their thinking with the class. As students respond, prompt them to explain what they learned about analyzing designs (strengths, weaknesses, mood boarding, audience fit, etc), why those insights matter when creating their own work, and how they might apply those ideas in future projects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that designers draw inspiration by analyzing what works and doesn’t work in existing designs, then intentionally applying those insights to guide their own style decisions.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90c3c012-db85-457a-b2c0-ece76d31a94d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "286637ee-7e0d-4ffc-8082-a13cb7e1ee8b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9b06d1-0b17-4f3e-b6bc-1b32c112c0b5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students experience how colors communicate tone and emotion by categorizing palettes based on mood."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9b06d1-0b17-4f3e-b6bc-1b32c112c0b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07b6b1c9-5aa7-489d-a025-b8152c0bec7a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Designers use color to make people feel something. Let's see what emotions these palettes create for you.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07b6b1c9-5aa7-489d-a025-b8152c0bec7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "007b89f8-de79-4a82-8576-78d7f35f2344",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show 4 color palettes. Ask students to label each of them with one of the categories (energetic, calming, playful, or luxury). Encourage quick, instinctive choices—there are no wrong answers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage gut reactions and reassure students that there are no wrong answers. The point is to notice how instinctively color communicates mood."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "007b89f8-de79-4a82-8576-78d7f35f2344",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d84838f4-86dc-4da2-9f5c-720a0fd21fb9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d84838f4-86dc-4da2-9f5c-720a0fd21fb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1617582-9485-4414-8d36-2f6e8d9e3cee",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Do these palettes fit the brands shown? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students recognize that colors communicate emotion and identity — and that meaning can change based on culture, audience, or context. Emphasize how color can reinforce or challenge a brand's identity and why inclusive design considers different interpretations.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students aren't familiar with a brand, have them focus on what the colors communicate: \"What mood or message do these colors send?\" or \"Do they feel consistent with the kind of product or audience this brand might have?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1617582-9485-4414-8d36-2f6e8d9e3cee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09b71a7c-5259-4329-b584-d549c3b9ba55",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at how powerful these colors are. Even without seeing the logo, you made strong guesses about what they meant. When we reveal the brands, sometimes your impressions match, and other times they don't. That shows how designers use color to shape how we see a brand — but color meanings can vary across cultures and accessibility needs. Even designers or AI might assume everyone sees color the same way, which isn't always true.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09b71a7c-5259-4329-b584-d549c3b9ba55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "637b7de4-dea8-4978-9293-6328d8aaa9d8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "637b7de4-dea8-4978-9293-6328d8aaa9d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5942a023-3e7c-465a-8ee3-4cb530a5199e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students analyze how real websites use fonts and colors to communicate brand identity and intended audience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the professional role of a marketing specialist to frame the lesson. Explain that students will take on this role as they analyze existing brands and create their own.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Brand Analysis",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Return to this role when introducing each new activity. Remind students that they're testing ideas and refining designs, just like professionals in real branding teams."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5942a023-3e7c-465a-8ee3-4cb530a5199e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b3a933c-3da3-474c-9f38-3305c77ea720",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b3a933c-3da3-474c-9f38-3305c77ea720",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd50df45-c822-4fce-aafe-00f517b6e5a7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1 to work with their partner and compare screenshots of websites in Sketch Lab. Students should record observations on color, typography, and then decided which audience is each brand aiming for.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure students are noting both fonts and colors. Push them past surface-level comments like \"Nike uses black.\" Instead, prompt: \"What feeling does this palette communicate?\" and \"What kind of user do you think they're trying to attract?\"\n",
        "progression_name": "Brand Analysis",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students are justifying their claims (e.g., \"Spotify uses playful green and rounded fonts, which feels approachable for younger users\")."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd50df45-c822-4fce-aafe-00f517b6e5a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c916e76-13e5-41fa-9d65-331fba3a04f5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c916e76-13e5-41fa-9d65-331fba3a04f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d390ddb-ea9d-4e02-9186-5f14ec62c001",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns did you notice in how brands use fonts and colors?*\n- *How did the design choices connect to audience?*\n- *What audiences might feel left out by these design choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify common design patterns (how fonts and colors communicate tone), connect those choices to the intended audience, and consider who might feel excluded or misrepresented by the same design choices. Encourage reasoning over preference—students explain why design decisions work or don't work for different audiences. If needed, model with examples: Bold sans-serif fonts and high-contrast colors often signal energy or athleticism, while muted serif fonts and gold tones feel more elegant or luxurious. For instance, Nike's dark palette feels powerful and competitive, while Spotify's rounded fonts and bright green feel youthful and approachable.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d390ddb-ea9d-4e02-9186-5f14ec62c001",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f150364c-5ecf-459c-ae4d-9ee96a497781",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f150364c-5ecf-459c-ae4d-9ee96a497781",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81a9d195-ed61-47c4-81af-e2bd646a7597",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just analyzed how real brands use colors and fonts to communicate to specific audiences. Those design decisions don’t happen randomly — they’re organized into something called a brand kit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81a9d195-ed61-47c4-81af-e2bd646a7597",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59ec211d-8bf8-4c73-8890-c808e02276b3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59ec211d-8bf8-4c73-8890-c808e02276b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a898878-fb5a-4265-8965-63f56717b198",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students design a mini brand identity that reflects a user persona's needs by making intentional, inclusive choices about colors and fonts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students get out their copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Create a Brand Kit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a898878-fb5a-4265-8965-63f56717b198",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ad554bc-c02e-44a4-aa60-6721b03bb019",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ad554bc-c02e-44a4-aa60-6721b03bb019",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b3e2682-ef6e-4a3c-8d47-ce158119bb5a",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define brand kit and direct students to record the definition in their Unit Guide. Briefly point out that a strong brand kit supports clarity, consistency, and accessibility in design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b3e2682-ef6e-4a3c-8d47-ce158119bb5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd9427d6-b435-446d-8d9a-24b3bcff125b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As marketing specialists, your job isn’t just to make something look good. A strong brand kit tells a clear story, stays consistent, and works for its audience. Before we build our own, let’s think about what that responsibility really means.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd9427d6-b435-446d-8d9a-24b3bcff125b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c14f61f1-25eb-4ed5-a526-cbaeec9c63a7",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c14f61f1-25eb-4ed5-a526-cbaeec9c63a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1d9eda7-c910-4b05-9780-3b7c10c80593",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If you were a marketing specialist, how would you make sure your brand kit works for many different users?*\n- *How might AI help you check your design before launching it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that a strong brand kit must be clear, readable, and inclusive for a range of users—not just visually appealing. Help them see that designers anticipate accessibility and bias concerns before launching, and that AI tools can be used to check and refine those decisions thoughtfully.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this discussion brief. The goal is awareness, not deep analysis. Let students know they’ll have access to an AI Brand Kit Helper if they want support checking accessibility or inclusivity—but they should first try to think through these considerations themselves."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1d9eda7-c910-4b05-9780-3b7c10c80593",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23a067ec-d151-43d0-8765-c6f519fea563",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23a067ec-d151-43d0-8765-c6f519fea563",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2091b56c-977b-4fa8-beff-e6722c15904a",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 2-3.\n- Level 2a: Students create a mini brand kit for a fictional product and user persona\n- Level 2b: Students chat about their brand kit with the AI Brand Kit Helper to check accessibility, inclusivity, or clarity.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students design, ask reflective questions: \"What mood do these colors communicate?\", \"Would these colors or fonts be easy for all users to read?\", or \"How does your design show awareness of audience or accessibility?\" Encourage students to use the AI Brand Kit Helper (Level 2b) to check accessibility and inclusivity. Remind them to reflect on the AI’s suggestions—decide which ones strengthen their design and why.\n",
        "progression_name": "Create a Brand Kit",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students the goal isn’t just to make something that looks good—it’s to design intentionally for their persona. If students struggle, have them choose one dominant mood word and build from there. If they finish early, challenge them to test an alternative color palette and compare which better fits their audience.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' brand kits as a formative check of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Apply design principles to create a cohesive visual identity.\n- Explain and justify how their choices communicate tone and audience.\n- Consider accessibility and inclusivity (e.g., color contrast, readability).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2091b56c-977b-4fa8-beff-e6722c15904a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7302df3c-5fa9-43b6-bfd7-db5c101396bc",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Brand Kit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7302df3c-5fa9-43b6-bfd7-db5c101396bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e133331-e7f2-4647-bc33-54782c9e208b",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e133331-e7f2-4647-bc33-54782c9e208b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b69d6ff2-9017-46e2-b73d-6d1ed5cf8cc4",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b69d6ff2-9017-46e2-b73d-6d1ed5cf8cc4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6600ed37-7f4a-43c6-b5a0-367cc5e67c49",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to present their brand kits to a peer group with the same fictional product. Each group explains their reasoning using the provided sentence frames. Encourage students to respond to peer feedback by explaining why they made a specific choice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for groups clearly tying color and typography choices to audience needs. If students only describe the palette, push with: \"Why does that choice matter for your persona?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "As groups present, listen for evidence that students can:\n- Explain how design choices communicate tone and identity.\n- Justify how their choices serve their user persona.\n- Acknowledge accessibility or inclusivity in their reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6600ed37-7f4a-43c6-b5a0-367cc5e67c49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfaf9f8b-bbd2-4f5a-bc5d-6af13b694c39",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfaf9f8b-bbd2-4f5a-bc5d-6af13b694c39",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc20152b-665c-418b-a295-fa7d8e6c31e8",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your design avoid stereotypes or bias?*\n- *How might users from different backgrounds interpret your color and font choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how design choices communicate identity and values while considering inclusivity and diverse perspectives. They connect visual decisions (color, font, tone) to ethical design—recognizing that different users may interpret designs differently based on culture, experience, or ability."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc20152b-665c-418b-a295-fa7d8e6c31e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6c4d1b0-6f8a-4931-bcbc-d6358b176a5c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them 2–3 minutes to write independently. Then invite a few volunteers to share. As they respond, press for connections to audience and purpose, accessibility and inclusivity, or intentional decision-making.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, highlight reflections that connect to:\n- Intentional design — making choices for a clear purpose and audience.\n- Accessibility and inclusivity — ensuring everyone can engage with the design.\n- Ethical awareness — understanding that both people and AI can carry bias into creative decisions.\n\nIf time allows, end with a short whole-class question: \"How can we design responsibly when we use AI to make creative choices?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6c4d1b0-6f8a-4931-bcbc-d6358b176a5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd6b462a-639b-4505-917f-4d4d1baac3a6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd6b462a-639b-4505-917f-4d4d1baac3a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6ff0fe2-af66-4b45-bb4f-97b5ecf61963",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6ff0fe2-af66-4b45-bb4f-97b5ecf61963",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1cc10781-8c2d-4c0a-872e-2a680a070d74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students quickly recall the difference between HTML and CSS by visualizing what happens when styling is removed. They connect this idea to brand clarity and user experience — realizing that CSS gives identity and consistency to a site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a webpage styled with CSS. Pose the question: \"If we removed the CSS stylesheet, what would be left?\" Students sketch a quick version of the same page without CSS and annotate their sketches with reasoning (e.g., \"Text still shows but without color,\" \"Images stack vertically,\" \"No clear brand identity\"). After sketching, have a few students share what they noticed. Encourage students to connect their observations to how CSS communicates tone and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for misconceptions like \"nothing will show.\" Prompt with: \"Would the content still exist without CSS?\" or \"What happens to layout and colors if CSS is gone?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does CSS turn a brand kit or design idea into a real web page?*\n- *What do developers and designers need to communicate clearly when styling a page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their understanding of CSS to real-world design workflows. The goal is to get students to recognize that CSS isn't just about color or font — it's about translating design decisions into code and ensuring collaboration between designers and developers."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1cc10781-8c2d-4c0a-872e-2a680a070d74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4913604d-744f-4fa8-82bb-568c20ae5899",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When a designer creates a layout or brand kit, it's not automatically ready for the web. CSS is how front-end developers bring those visuals to life — they translate colors, spacing, and typography into code. This handoff between design and development is part of what you'll practice today — testing how well AI follows design instructions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4913604d-744f-4fa8-82bb-568c20ae5899",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59c8a2b4-ca85-4333-b1a1-fa62aa2778ea",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59c8a2b4-ca85-4333-b1a1-fa62aa2778ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "725b8d5c-cbcb-4f21-974a-49cf08d9f7f8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Establish students' role as front-end web designers who translate brand kits into live, coded web pages using CSS.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will act as front-end designers using AI as their coding partner. Designers define the vision, and front-end developers ensure it's executed correctly in code — responsibly, accessibly, and on brand.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Sheets",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Refer back to this role throughout the activity. Remind students they're testing and refining AI-generated CSS like professionals validating a build. Remind students that AI can generate CSS syntax quickly, but it does not understand their specific brand or user needs. Their job is to define the intent and evaluate whether the output reflects it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "725b8d5c-cbcb-4f21-974a-49cf08d9f7f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3de630aa-00f3-43ae-bbc5-5b45a7afad8f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define style sheet. Explain that CSS is the bridge between design and development: it's how the decisions made in a brand kit — like colors, fonts, and layout — become consistent code. Every time students see a website where the colors, fonts, and spacing look intentional and consistent, that's CSS at work.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students apply a stylesheet across multiple pages to maintain consistent brand identity, testing and refining AI-generated code for accuracy and readability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students learn how to add images as context for their AI prompts.\n- Level 2: Students learn how to add code as context for their AI prompts.\n- Level 3: Students learn how to add items to and retrieve items from the Backpack.\n- Level 4: Students learn how to add images to their webpage.",
        "progression_name": "How To: Adding Context and Saving Prompts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3de630aa-00f3-43ae-bbc5-5b45a7afad8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab2c1929-103b-4431-8df7-2d49b5537500",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly remind students that they practiced this “Prompt Like a Developer” routine in Lesson 3. Review the three requirements and explain that they’ll use the same structure again in the next level before prompting AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 5 through 7.\n- Level 5: Students draft a structured prompt using goal, constraints, and edge cases before prompting AI.\n- Level 6: Students prompt AI to generate and apply a basic CSS stylesheet.\n- Level 7: Students prompt AI to apply brand colors and fonts to key regions (header, nav, main, footer) and refine for accuracy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students provided the stylesheet as context to the AI and prompted the AI to generate new HTML with their stylesheet linked in the <head>). Have students explain what they notice about the AI's code and why it made those styling choices.",
        "progression_name": "Apply a Stylesheet",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to \"debug like a developer.\" If the AI's output doesn't look right, they should describe why instead of just retrying the prompt. This models safe, reflective use of AI tools — not just trust in automation. If time allows, connect back to the Front-End Designer role: \"This is the testing phase — where developers validate the AI's work before the site goes live.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab2c1929-103b-4431-8df7-2d49b5537500",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb19e8c6-032e-459a-a171-d7d33faf20d8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did the AI change in your CSS — and why?*\n- *Did it make any styling choices you wouldn't have made yourself?*\n- *How do you know if its output matches your brand goals?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how AI interprets prompts and makes automated styling decisions. They consider when AI-generated CSS aligns with their intent and when it diverges. The goal is for students to see themselves as responsible reviewers, not passive users — understanding that AI can enhance efficiency but still requires human judgment for accuracy, accessibility, and design intent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb19e8c6-032e-459a-a171-d7d33faf20d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "334832a8-868c-44f9-ab42-48736fbf0c38",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When developers use AI tools, they don't assume every output is correct — they check what changed, why it changed, and whether it still fits the brand's tone and user needs. You're doing the same thing here: validating the AI's choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "334832a8-868c-44f9-ab42-48736fbf0c38",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9eb75f7b-e630-4c51-95f4-156f04da9d5c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students demonstrate mastery by refining and experimenting with CSS using AI assistance — improving accuracy, readability, and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students go to Level 8 and choose one challenge to complete. Encourage them to think like developers in a testing phase: What small refinements could make your page more polished, consistent, or accessible?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they:\n- Clearly define what they are improving before prompting AI.\n- Make one change at a time and preview before layering additional edits.\n- Understand what their AI-generated CSS is doing before changing it.\n- Document what changed and why it improved the design.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Choice Challenge",
        "progression_name": "Styling Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the Level 9 reflection level to formatively assess whether students can:\n- Describe one specific styling refinement they made.\n- Explain how they used AI intentionally (including how they adjusted their prompt, if needed).\n- Connect their change to improved brand alignment and usability.\n- Distinguish between structural changes (HTML) and styling changes (CSS).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9eb75f7b-e630-4c51-95f4-156f04da9d5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e47d35e9-a804-4d15-b9cf-7b9c8891a5e1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to reflect on their learning by responding to the prompts on the slide. Ask them to share quick responses out loud or record them on sticky notes, a shared board, or a digital tool. Help students connect today's lesson to the larger story of how design becomes code. They should recognize that CSS translates brand identity into real web pages, and that AI acts as a coding assistant, not an automatic expert.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e47d35e9-a804-4d15-b9cf-7b9c8891a5e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29186c86-16a2-4bdd-ab82-8feff4487c7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e1a6a43-01e8-4f17-afb1-bda1b734d847",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Have students activate empathy by connecting unmet technology needs with emotions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of a time when technology didn't meet your needs. In one word, how did it make you feel?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students see that unmet needs carry emotional weight (frustration, confusion, being left out) and that emotions are part of user needs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e1a6a43-01e8-4f17-afb1-bda1b734d847",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23072a29-d20a-4c53-93dc-8d9e52098830"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce6cf2f8-df50-4314-a1e3-1f42cad0373c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we build personas and problem statements today, we want to capture not just what users do, but how they feel. If we understand needs and emotions clearly, we can design solutions that don't leave users frustrated.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce6cf2f8-df50-4314-a1e3-1f42cad0373c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23072a29-d20a-4c53-93dc-8d9e52098830"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bf34182-3a70-4c2d-aa06-b45c402ba285",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Analyze a persona from a series and connect it to design implications.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give each student a copy of the Design Implications activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Read an informational panel on how user personas inform design decisions\n- Level 2: Analyze a sample user persona, inferring design decisions and connecting them to a problem statement. Complete page 1 of the activity guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "User Persona Analysis",
        "progression_name": "Build a User Personas and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bf34182-3a70-4c2d-aa06-b45c402ba285",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "167122eb-59d1-49b3-9efc-775d6b250a01",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "User Persona Analysis: Alex"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "167122eb-59d1-49b3-9efc-775d6b250a01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dab912cf-9d2e-4877-b065-e404ad591735",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define problem statement. Problem statement format - [user] needs a way to [need] because [reason].\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which statement is stronger? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that B is better because it's specific, user-centered, and explains constraints."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dab912cf-9d2e-4877-b065-e404ad591735",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f6abb35-06ab-4c26-b41c-9bff694a9961",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students create a user persona and write problem statements with AI support.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 3. Students should choose a scenario, create a sample user persona, and draft a problem statement.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Create a User Persona",
        "progression_name": "Create a User Persona and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f6abb35-06ab-4c26-b41c-9bff694a9961",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe2bd43f-bde3-470f-a442-4c42768804c7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students swap personas and problem statements with a partner to provide feedback. Students use a Peer Review Checklist to provide feedback.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for feedback that is specific and actionable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 4 and 5.\n- Level 4: Review the vocabulary term problem statement.\n- Level 5: Define the term in student's own words. Use examples from the lesson to explain their thinking.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe2bd43f-bde3-470f-a442-4c42768804c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79ebfce7-74dc-4b05-9813-7538692e806b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79ebfce7-74dc-4b05-9813-7538692e806b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "04fb4b33-cdd6-4271-a5ba-d51390749296"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14a19331-34a1-430f-a2ad-5105f30b50ea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students practice decomposition by organizing messy information into a clear, logical structure before learning the term site map.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two or three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of the Website cards."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14a19331-34a1-430f-a2ad-5105f30b50ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5d5d9851-f319-4d14-abd2-8606cab00e50",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Your client's website is confusing! Users can't find what they need. How might you reorganize the pages so that navigation feels clear and logical?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5d5d9851-f319-4d14-abd2-8606cab00e50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa4036e8-dd93-4b29-ba6d-5dbb33e06c05",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to arrange the 12 page cards into a logical structure, using arrows to show how users would move through the site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move between groups and ask why they grouped pages a certain way. Encourage reasoning such as \"We put Contact under About because users usually look for it with company info.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students know that the example on the slide is not the \"correct answer.\" There's no single right way to organize a site — what matters is whether the structure makes sense for the user. Encourage groups to explain their reasoning and compare different logical choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa4036e8-dd93-4b29-ba6d-5dbb33e06c05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bea6ba45-6a29-4696-8935-03eedd4e5bd0",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bea6ba45-6a29-4696-8935-03eedd4e5bd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bfae031d-7b78-451d-9eef-8ffdf64b001b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define site map. Explain that site maps help us see the overall structure and logic of a site — not just the pages themselves but how they relate. Tell students that now that they have explored what makes navigation clear, they will create their own structured site map later in today's activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns do we notice across site maps?*\n  - *Where did most groups put Home?*\n  - *Where did most groups put Contact?*\n  - *How did groups handle sections or sub-pages?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to surface shared principles of effective site maps, such as: clear hierarchy (Home at the top), consistent placement of key pages, logical grouping of related content. Capture a few criteria on the board for reference in later activities.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention unusual or creative structures, validate them — use these examples to discuss when breaking convention can help or hurt usability."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bfae031d-7b78-451d-9eef-8ffdf64b001b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a210c99d-e6d0-4d6b-a31f-4b5941d1cd25",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a210c99d-e6d0-4d6b-a31f-4b5941d1cd25",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37c4d390-898b-4bac-b076-fc4db9d0dc11",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37c4d390-898b-4bac-b076-fc4db9d0dc11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "747e1147-6932-4462-beff-7db8b4ff64ed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Connect site mapping to real-world roles in tech and show how UX Designers plan user experiences before coding begins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly spotlight careers such as UX Designer or Information Architect. Explain that these roles are responsible for structuring site navigation and ensuring users can find what they need. Emphasize that today's activity mirrors that real-world process — planning first, building later.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "User Experience Design",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name an app or website that feels easy to navigate. Ask, \"What do you think the designer did to make that happen?\" This grounds the role in tangible examples students know."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "747e1147-6932-4462-beff-7db8b4ff64ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4f2d316-44f1-4e8d-a7e5-5fce203ce87b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4f2d316-44f1-4e8d-a7e5-5fce203ce87b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "529108a6-9383-492b-bd11-1c60e48bafd6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As UX Designers, part of your job is planning how users move through a site to complete a goal — that's called a user flow. For example, if you're designing a food delivery app, the user flow might be: Home → Menu → Add to Cart → Checkout. Today, you'll create your own user flow for your app idea to make sure navigation makes sense before anything is built.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "529108a6-9383-492b-bd11-1c60e48bafd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8668fbf-ca41-418e-829b-46532091d801",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user flow. Explain that they'll map one today as part of their site map challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 7.\n- Levels 1 and 2: Students create a site map and trace a user flow for a chosen persona.\n- Levels 3 through 6: Students read and explain the key terms site map and user flow.\n- Level 7: Students respond to a final reflection or Check for Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that groups are creating nested structures rather than flat lists. Prompt them with questions like, \"Would a user know where to find this page?\" and \"Does this structure help your persona reach their goal easily?\"",
        "progression_name": "Create a Site Map and User Flow"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8668fbf-ca41-418e-829b-46532091d801",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9366881c-b43e-4b7c-9180-7ad99824b5f0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- Remind students to consider their persona's goals and challenges as they trace the flow. Does the flow make sense for that user?\n- Remind students that there isn't one \"right\" site structure — what matters is how well it serves their persona's needs. Encourage them to explain why their flow makes sense for that user."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use levels 1 and 2 to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Creating a site map showing page hierarchy and navigation\n- Tracing a possible user flow across a web app."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9366881c-b43e-4b7c-9180-7ad99824b5f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "217cba01-e4f7-4c26-b6f9-e3bd98d6948f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "217cba01-e4f7-4c26-b6f9-e3bd98d6948f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "147aa371-ffc6-406f-962a-974b01ce8856",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before we hand our site maps to the AI, let's think like designers and engineers. When we give instructions to an AI, it only knows what we tell it — it doesn't automatically understand our intent. Let's talk about how that affects the way we explain our designs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "147aa371-ffc6-406f-962a-974b01ce8856",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d44ff256-5f54-4b13-aadb-018a830b22a0",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your site map make navigation clear and support user needs?*\n- *How do we explain our site's logic to AI so it builds what we intend?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to connect human-centered design and AI collaboration. Students should recognize that:\n- A clear site map helps users navigate and achieve goals easily.\n- Communicating that structure clearly helps the AI generate more accurate, user-friendly designs.\n\nEncourage students to explain their reasoning using examples: \"We put Help under Contact because that's where users expect it.\" and \"If we don't tell the AI why a section is important, it might skip it or place it wrong.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask one more additional question: What do you need to make sure the AI understands before it starts building? This primes students for precision and transparency — a great segue into the next task."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d44ff256-5f54-4b13-aadb-018a830b22a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cb6ca96-21e8-4eb2-82a4-69e1ed9a3b91",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cb6ca96-21e8-4eb2-82a4-69e1ed9a3b91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8aa757d5-1214-4e6a-aff3-f70fdb3f50d7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Students apply their site map and user flow to generate a working, multi-page website using AI. They practice writing clear, structured prompts that communicate their logic and verify whether the AI builds what they intended.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Have students complete Level 8. Explain that this is their AI hand-off phase—they'll take their plan and test whether the AI can interpret it accurately just like a UX Designer communicating with a development team. Remind them that AI doesn't \"see\" their reasoning—it can only follow what they describe clearly in the prompt. Encourage students to revise their prompt if something doesn't look right — this models iteration and precision in AI collaboration.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Prompt students with questions that build reflection and clarity: \"Does your website structure match your site map?\", \"What might you reword in your prompt to make the AI's output clearer?\", or \"If a link doesn't work, what does that tell you about how the AI interpreted your instructions?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Articulate for AI",
        "progression_name": "Build Your Web App with AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Show or read aloud the optional sample prompt to model precision. Emphasize that this isn't a script — students can adapt the wording as long as they're describing structure, navigation, and clarity."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this level to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Translate design logic into precise, testable AI prompts (clarity and structure in communication).\n- Evaluate AI output for accuracy and alignment with their original site map and user flow.\n- Iterate thoughtfully — making targeted prompt revisions based on observed gaps or misinterpretations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8aa757d5-1214-4e6a-aff3-f70fdb3f50d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ecc76e16-8300-4916-982d-dd7e33511fc8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When we prompted the AI, how did we make sure it understood our site's logic?*\n- *What made your structure easier or harder for the AI to get right?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to reflect on what made their site map clear and how effectively they communicated that logic to the AI. Students should recognize that:\n- Clear, organized structure helps both users and AI follow site logic.\n- Precise prompts and explicit relationships (like navigation links) improve AI accuracy.\n- Iterating on prompts is part of refining and testing how well AI interprets design intent.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ecc76e16-8300-4916-982d-dd7e33511fc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bc8c091a-ff58-474f-8359-57a4a0d380b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c22a64c6-9423-4e72-97b9-ff33ebcbf990",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build empathy for users with different visual abilities and recognize how design choices—like color, contrast, and text size—impact accessibility. They connect these challenges to how both human designers and AI tools must think differently to create inclusive web apps."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c22a64c6-9423-4e72-97b9-ff33ebcbf990",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a01e198-7a5e-4180-b026-d35817918be3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we'll step into the shoes of users who see the web differently and explore how both people and AI systems can design for accessibility.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a01e198-7a5e-4180-b026-d35817918be3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5da2349c-3a1d-4300-9b6b-ab2387ea71a9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Low Vision Experience\" and “Color Blindness Experience” videos.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What stood out about the challenges these users faced?*\n- *What mistakes might AI make if it doesn't consider these challenges?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify specific usability barriers, such as difficulty reading small or low-contrast text, confusing navigation, or reliance on color alone. Students should explain how these issues affect a user's experience (for example, not being able to find a button or read important text). Students should recognize that AI might reproduce inaccessible patterns — for instance, choosing poor color contrast, omitting alt text, or generating layouts that assume perfect vision.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can also provide students with the link to Project Lima and have them experience these accessibility issues firsthand. Have students choose at least one of the three experiences to try. Encourage them to spend a few minutes exploring the chosen experience(s). Observe students as they engage with the simulations. After exploring, ask: What was the hardest part of that experience? or How did that simulation make you feel as a user? Connect their emotions to the earlier questions about AI and design responsibility."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5da2349c-3a1d-4300-9b6b-ab2387ea71a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ba9b7fa-06a2-4c69-9140-200b28f3c0cd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ba9b7fa-06a2-4c69-9140-200b28f3c0cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "065f87af-df88-49cc-8c0a-e7ea6957ed5c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "065f87af-df88-49cc-8c0a-e7ea6957ed5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb46e8e6-1cf5-47ba-aea5-23be69181d45",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students are introduced to the accessibility challenges that exist across the web and learn how those issues influence AI-generated designs. They explore the real-world role of the Inclusive Designer and understand why human judgment is essential in guiding AI toward more accessible outcomes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the key stat aloud to emphasize its scale. Then, highlight the examples of common issues (low contrast text, missing alt text, unlabeled forms, empty links/buttons).",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "How Accessible is the Web?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb46e8e6-1cf5-47ba-aea5-23be69181d45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63e7bb2c-2c47-481e-9f04-92e0f9752851",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI design tools are trained on the same web we just looked at, which means they inherit its strengths and its flaws. Let's talk about what that could mean for accessibility.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63e7bb2c-2c47-481e-9f04-92e0f9752851",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30942fb9-6204-4bfb-950c-eab89f290c60",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If most websites have accessibility issues, what might AI learn from that data?*\n- *How could those patterns show up in AI-generated layouts or code?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI systems learn from existing examples on the web. If most of those examples are inaccessible, the AI will also learn and reproduce those same barriers. Students should start connecting data quality to AI output quality. Students should identify concrete ways inaccessibility could appear in AI work. Students should see that AI can generate polished-looking designs that still fail real users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Inclusive Designer role.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the “Thinking Accessibility in UX Design” video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30942fb9-6204-4bfb-950c-eab89f290c60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb16cf5e-81ba-435f-be4c-aefb04969ae1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll get to practice thinking like an Inclusive Designer. You'll look at an AI-generated webpage, test it for accessibility, and then use what you find to guide the AI toward better, more inclusive design choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb16cf5e-81ba-435f-be4c-aefb04969ae1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "646d7a0a-1649-4d92-acff-f343a3547802",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how to use the POUR framework (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) to describe and organize accessibility issues they observed in AI-generated layouts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student/pair of students a copy of the Accessibility in Design activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define the POUR Principles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Levels 1 and 2: Students review and define POUR Principle.\n- Level 3: Students identify at least two to three accessibility issues on the provided web page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they examine the page. Prompt them with questions like: 'What POUR principle does this issue relate to?' or 'How might this issue affect a user with low vision?' Ensure they are focusing on issues like low contrast text, missing alt text, and tiny buttons. Ensure students use the Accessibility Prompt Builder in their Unit Guide to write AI prompts that suggest fixes for the issues they identified.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "The POUR Principle"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "646d7a0a-1649-4d92-acff-f343a3547802",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65f49e24-d3db-4cbb-acc2-de7e0447cea1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: POUR Principles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65f49e24-d3db-4cbb-acc2-de7e0447cea1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53d0f84e-a9c7-47c2-9e16-4e0882bdd3a4",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Fix Accessibility Issues",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students were absent for the previous lesson where they created site maps/apps, make sure they are aware of the AI Prompt in the instructions that will provide a site map for them. Emphasize that the goal is not necessarily to 'solve' every issue, but to practice identifying problems and communicating them effectively through prompts. This is a key skill for working with AI and for accessibility design in general."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53d0f84e-a9c7-47c2-9e16-4e0882bdd3a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a957d2c4-7506-4727-b67f-50fc37f009db",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a957d2c4-7506-4727-b67f-50fc37f009db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd913774-0194-4540-a189-6dce8385e60b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why do we need a framework like POUR when designing for accessibility?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that POUR provides a structured approach to ensure digital products are usable by everyone. Guide them to see it as a checklist or a set of guiding principles that help designers systematically consider different aspects of accessibility. Emphasize that it helps ensure inclusivity and usability across a wide range of user needs and abilities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd913774-0194-4540-a189-6dce8385e60b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "433a4c94-8701-42d4-b508-86981dfcb0a7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now we'll use these principles to start identifying issues and prompting AI to fix them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "433a4c94-8701-42d4-b508-86981dfcb0a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97961015-fc2a-438d-ad78-2382993d71dd",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4. Students apply accessibility principles to their own apps from Lesson 8 by revisiting them with a user persona who has an accessibility constraint.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Remind them to be specific in their prompts, referencing the POUR principles. Ask students to explain how their user's constraint makes certain aspects of their app challenging. Prompt them to connect specific design elements to potential accessibility barriers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 5."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97961015-fc2a-438d-ad78-2382993d71dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8252879a-218a-4d83-a560-d4d85c0a010d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply Accessibility Principles"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8252879a-218a-4d83-a560-d4d85c0a010d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4eb39b41-cf4c-471e-8fd8-54286965668b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to connect their suggested change to the idea of empathy and how would this change make life easier for their user."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This prompt can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of applying the POUR framework and user-centered design principles."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4eb39b41-cf4c-471e-8fd8-54286965668b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f82b4de-d2f8-4331-be82-fae001199b5d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f82b4de-d2f8-4331-be82-fae001199b5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd417ef1-11b5-466f-b064-4b37dafa8609"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d240ec2-c420-4436-a8cc-ec313e55839f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d240ec2-c420-4436-a8cc-ec313e55839f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd417ef1-11b5-466f-b064-4b37dafa8609"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1494fe9-3302-4722-bdd6-cca685175003",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience the purpose of quick, low-fidelity sketches by focusing on structure and function rather than visual design. This reinforces abstraction (ignoring unnecessary details), decomposition (breaking the page into parts), and pattern recognition (noticing common layouts)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1494fe9-3302-4722-bdd6-cca685175003",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58208ff8-5047-4657-9285-d713c57bdc34",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Designers often start by sketching rough layouts to plan how information will be organized. The goal right now is speed and clarity — not polished design. Focus on the big pieces that make a homepage work: navigation, sections, and where content will go.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58208ff8-5047-4657-9285-d713c57bdc34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0483791b-70f6-4719-a7f3-e72cb97bc7b9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough home page for a fictional music playlist app. It should include basic navigation, key sections, a short paragraph, and an image gallery.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize speed over perfection. Encourage students to use rough lines, boxes, and placeholders. They should avoid details like colors, specific photos, or exact wording. This keeps attention on structure — a core idea in abstraction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0483791b-70f6-4719-a7f3-e72cb97bc7b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b50fb357-8b35-4632-b295-e925cc0643d9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b50fb357-8b35-4632-b295-e925cc0643d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23948cc9-e7c9-43d8-8ee0-03c85adee88f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough About page for the same fictional music playlist app. The page should include a short description of the app, a section for team or creator information, and navigation that links back to the homepage.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students sketch the second page, ask them to reuse layout patterns — designers look for consistency across pages to improve navigation and flow. Remind students that they are focusing on structure and layout, not visual design — this continues the practice of abstraction (ignoring details that don't affect structure) and decomposition (breaking each page into functional sections)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23948cc9-e7c9-43d8-8ee0-03c85adee88f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1741c777-b1ab-4a83-8610-44fd193840be",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1741c777-b1ab-4a83-8610-44fd193840be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24643ebb-c037-454c-a969-0b064d2e8c76",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What choices did you make about what to include or leave out?*\n- *Which parts of your sketch feel like human design choices, and which could AI handle later?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to reflect on how they made structural decisions (abstraction) and how these choices connect to the designer's role in a human-in-the-loop workflow. Students should recognize that:\n- Sketching focuses on what's essential for structure and function, not polished visuals.\n- Designers make early layout and content decisions before AI or developers build — that's what keeps humans in control of the creative process."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24643ebb-c037-454c-a969-0b064d2e8c76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3591223f-8ab0-4254-8640-97e3315adb80",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When designers start a project, they make big decisions about structure and function — not about visuals or colors. That's because AI or developers can handle those details later. What you just created is actually your first wireframe — a quick sketch that helps plan structure before any visuals or code come into play. Let's take a look at what wireframes include — and what they leave out.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3591223f-8ab0-4254-8640-97e3315adb80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b3f459e-13a0-404b-afbe-a297dc50eb68",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define wireframe as a low-fidelity, structural layout showing content and functionality without colors, images, or visual styling. Use the blueprint analogy: just like an architect sketches a blueprint before building, designers create wireframes to plan structure and layout before adding visuals. Explain that this step keeps humans in the loop — designers decide structure and logic, while AI or developers handle styling and details later.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students what other kinds of jobs use \"blueprints\" or rough drafts before building the final version. This helps them connect the idea of planning structure first — a core design principle — to the broader concept of human decision-making before automation."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b3f459e-13a0-404b-afbe-a297dc50eb68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fa6a95c-e3d0-4001-8542-1367131e6de1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fa6a95c-e3d0-4001-8542-1367131e6de1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac154cab-acab-4a46-a46c-708e45535a4d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac154cab-acab-4a46-a46c-708e45535a4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23059e05-9a98-4646-9748-63040a889557",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students create low-fidelity wireframes for multiple pages of a single app, ensuring consistency in key UI elements. They map a logical user flow and think like UX Designers deciding structure before AI builds.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the video \"UI Design: When to Wireframe\"\n\nQuestions to consider with video:\n- Why do UX Designers start with sketches before designing?\n- What problems could skipping this step create later?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students understand that wireframing is about clarity and complexity, not polish. Students should recognize that:\n- Pros use sketches to think through structure and user flow before design.\n- Wireframing saves time by revealing layout or usability issues early.\n- This early planning is a human decision step — designers make structure decisions before AI or tools automate visuals.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display both the high-fidelity mockup and a low-fidelity wireframe of the same web app with extras removed. Facilitate a brief discussion about which would be easier to change and why. Explain that designers sketch in low fidelity because it's faster and easier to adjust structure before visuals are added. This step keeps humans in control of key decisions before AI or developers build.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23059e05-9a98-4646-9748-63040a889557",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e5e0af6-b615-4ee4-95ba-784a3c011cd0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You're the UX Designer deciding structure before AI or developers build. Your job is to make the layout clear and consistent so that anyone — or any AI tool — could understand how the app works. That's part of keeping humans in the loop.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e5e0af6-b615-4ee4-95ba-784a3c011cd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "680e9565-15c8-4f93-94ee-76c65da3ae5d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students create two wireframes each for a chosen a web app idea in Sketch Lab\n- Level 2: Students read the vocabulary definition for wireframe.\n- Level 3: Students add their own explanation or example of wireframe to the vocab free response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students have chosen an app idea and understand its basic purpose. Look for consistent navigation and layout across pages. Prompt pairs to discuss why consistency is important for user experience. If groups struggle, remind them that AI can assist later with text or visuals — but humans design the structure first. Ask probing questions about how consistency helps users move through their app or why their layout make sense for their user persona.",
        "progression_name": "Wireframe an App Idea"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "680e9565-15c8-4f93-94ee-76c65da3ae5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a627c77-c0b1-4c15-a851-7e7292c30472",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Wireframe",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize consistency across wireframes — this reinforces pattern recognition. If time allows, have students later prompt AI to generate a sample layout and compare it with their designs. This makes the human vs. AI interpretation explicit and previews upcoming AI lessons."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 1 to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Create a low-fidelity wireframe for their web app's pages.\n- Map a user journey across pages in their wireframes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a627c77-c0b1-4c15-a851-7e7292c30472",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b5c034e-56c2-427d-9f30-838f404acf3f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b5c034e-56c2-427d-9f30-838f404acf3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed265a9f-dbae-42e2-89ca-4a43fc86bf97",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it's time to share your work and get feedback from your classmates. Remember — your goal isn't just to hear what they like, but to learn how clearly your design communicates structure and flow.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed265a9f-dbae-42e2-89ca-4a43fc86bf97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "577f5bcc-c787-45a3-bb01-3e53000d9224",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs share their user journey and wireframes with another pair. Students place sticky-note comments using the sentence starters. Encourage students to focus on clarity and usability, not visuals or polish.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen to the feedback being exchanged. Ensure students are providing specific and constructive feedback. Guide them if their feedback is too vague or not helpful.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use peer feedback to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Evaluate and explain how clearly a wireframe communicates user flow.\n- Give and receive peer feedback on wireframe clarity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "577f5bcc-c787-45a3-bb01-3e53000d9224",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b2ea0f5-fc3d-4154-a850-a1e7980b7fa6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b2ea0f5-fc3d-4154-a850-a1e7980b7fa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe541a9e-1487-4cfd-bbde-6df5f7839f3b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does wireframing make design work faster and clearer?*\n- *What design decisions must always stay human?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Encourage students to recognize that wireframing improves both clarity and efficiency by allowing designers to plan structure and fix problems early, before visuals or coding begin. Students should also understand that some decisions—like layout, usability, and user flow—must remain human choices because they require reasoning, empathy, and ethical awareness. This conversation reinforces the idea that humans lead the creative and structural stages of design, while AI can assist later with details and automation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe541a9e-1487-4cfd-bbde-6df5f7839f3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a35f6492-77a7-4cf9-9d8b-78322a260f6d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students reflect on \"Glows\", \"Grows\", and \"Want to Knows\".\n- Glows: Which choices helped make your wireframe clear and easy to understand — for users or for AI later in the process?\n- Grows: What's one thing you could improve next time you wireframe?\n- Want-to-Knows: What questions do you still have about wireframing or the design process?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a35f6492-77a7-4cf9-9d8b-78322a260f6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6987f845-0307-46f4-95e1-cb23334e7b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a744ef39-5994-4222-aade-28fc67621dd6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a744ef39-5994-4222-aade-28fc67621dd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6987f845-0307-46f4-95e1-cb23334e7b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4434fab1-54f0-4982-a9c6-b6b52b788bdc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that combining structure (a map) with instructions (directions) leads to clearer results—introducing the idea that AI works best when given both a wireframe and a prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which would you rather have if you were trying to find a new place?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reason that having both written directions and a map makes it easier to understand and follow a plan because it provides structure and context. Listen for students noting that each on its own can be confusing or incomplete — setting up the idea that combining both gives clearer guidance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4434fab1-54f0-4982-a9c6-b6b52b788bdc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd4d9e6a-e105-4ccd-baf1-b0f4f6d7a066",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Most people pick both, because the map gives structure and the directions explain the steps. The two work better together than alone. A map shows the big picture — where you are and where you're going — while the directions tell you how to get there. Keep that idea in mind, because today we'll see how the same thing applies when you work with AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd4d9e6a-e105-4ccd-baf1-b0f4f6d7a066",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9df48723-7f16-46e1-8689-cb4963e758a3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the wireframe and written prompt help the AI understand what to build."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9df48723-7f16-46e1-8689-cb4963e758a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc2cc245-2aaf-4417-887c-3793d921f0f8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just like maps and directions, your wireframe and prompt each do something different but complementary. The wireframe gives structure — it's the visual plan that shows where everything belongs on the page. The prompt adds meaning — it tells the AI what each part is for and what kind of content should go there. When you give AI both, it understands not just what to build, but why each piece matters.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc2cc245-2aaf-4417-887c-3793d921f0f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d211701-47c5-4ccc-9f46-a4daaff9df9f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of things might the AI understand or do better when it also has your wireframe as context?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reason that giving AI visual context (like a wireframe) helps it make smarter, more aligned design choices — beyond just structure. Listen for ideas like:\n- matching the layout and proportions to their plan\n- choosing color schemes or images that fit the page's purpose\n- organizing content logically based on the design\n- generating code that's easier to adjust or style later\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d211701-47c5-4ccc-9f46-a4daaff9df9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed08db48-f3e6-40a8-81f1-093f71598511",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students generate their first AI-created layout from a wireframe using a structured prompt. They compare the AI output to their wireframe, identify matches and mismatches, and begin refining their process based on what they observe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the From Wireframes to Layouts activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 1. Explain that they'll upload their wireframe from Lesson 10 and prompt the AI using the structured recipe.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe how students reason through the comparison. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"Where did the AI follow your wireframe well?\"\n- \"What doesn't match, and why do you think that happened?\"\n- \"What details might you add or change next time to get a closer match?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Generate Using Wireframes",
        "progression_name": "Generate from a Wireframe",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If the AI's layout misses the mark, remind students they can clarify their prompt, add specific layout details, or generate smaller sections separately to get better results."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed08db48-f3e6-40a8-81f1-093f71598511",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "034528c2-bc4a-4fba-8273-7c5191632532",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "034528c2-bc4a-4fba-8273-7c5191632532",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f6fb15b-f830-49b2-bb72-9a45a347e267",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Did the AI omit any parts of your wireframe or miss user needs when generating your layout?*\n- *What might have caused that, and how did you adjust your prompt or code to fix it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that some omissions may come from unclear prompts, while others reflect the AI's own limitations. Listen for students identifying specific mismatches between their wireframe and the generated layout and considering whether those issues came from how they prompted or from how the AI interpreted the task. Students should discuss possible causes for mismatches, like vague wording, missing details, or AI misunderstanding structure. They should describe how they iterated to improve results. Listen for students showing accountability (clarifying or refining their prompt) while also recognizing when AI-generated outputs require human correction or more guidance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f6fb15b-f830-49b2-bb72-9a45a347e267",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b07a6727-159a-48bd-94ef-061d9b944b1f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students refine their AI-generated layout by identifying targeted fixes, improving accessibility using the POUR checklist, and applying debugging strategies to resolve persistent issues. They iterate through multiple versions to move closer to a complete, functional design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 2. Explain that this stage focuses on refining their layout and debugging issues that prevent it from matching their plan or being accessible. Students will upload their wireframe and use prompts to generate, refine, and debug their layout in Web Lab. They should use the Prompt Recipe Sheet, Refinement Checklist, and Debugging Checklist from their Unit Guide to help them iterate until the AI output matches their wireframe as closely as possible.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to verbalize their reasoning:\n- \"What specific fix are you targeting in this version?\"\n- \"Which POUR principle did you apply, and why?\"\n- \"What debugging strategy are you trying, and how is it helping?\"\n\nListen for students describing how they test and refine their prompts systematically.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Generate Using Wireframes",
        "progression_name": "Refine, Debug, Iterate",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to view each version as an experiment that brings them closer to their intended design and improves their collaboration with AI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b07a6727-159a-48bd-94ef-061d9b944b1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a464937-69c2-4b14-a586-48439f336030",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How has your AI build workflow evolved now that you're translating wireframes into full layouts?*\n- *What's your responsibility when the AI's layout doesn't match your plan or your users' needs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should describe how their workflow has become more intentional. Listen for students explaining that they now use wireframes as input to guide the AI, include clear goals or context in their prompts, and make targeted refinements to align structure with their plan. They should recognize that they're learning to communicate design intent more effectively to the AI. Students should identify that they are responsible for ensuring the final layout aligns with their design and user needs. Listen for students discussing how they revise prompts, debug or adjust code, and apply design principles to fix mismatches.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the check for understanding on Level 3.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses to gauge understanding of debugging and refinement. Consider displaying anonymous student responses on an interactive whiteboard to drive a class debrief."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a464937-69c2-4b14-a586-48439f336030",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ba43c2e-a088-431c-97eb-cfd38e5f359f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ba43c2e-a088-431c-97eb-cfd38e5f359f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97c3c98d-c0de-4928-aae2-63003824c35a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fad44d7-1152-4851-9273-959114635bb6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fad44d7-1152-4851-9273-959114635bb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97c3c98d-c0de-4928-aae2-63003824c35a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09295084-9bd4-4f74-b1e9-ae47b4b846fd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see firsthand how layouts change across screen sizes and start noticing breakpoints and issues."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09295084-9bd4-4f74-b1e9-ae47b4b846fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "559b0b05-8fbe-40c7-98d0-05f1de3bf2e8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to become 'Resize Detectives' to explore how websites adapt to different screen sizes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "559b0b05-8fbe-40c7-98d0-05f1de3bf2e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23c22b73-d11e-4517-a05d-2682de345f1d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show students the video of website resizing. After watching the video, ask:\n- What changes?\n- What breaks?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If possible, open a sample website and do a live demo of shrinking the browser window from desktop width down to tablet and then phone width. Ask students, \"How should layouts adapt across devices?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23c22b73-d11e-4517-a05d-2682de345f1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "733a01cd-bc9b-4f62-8bff-b83aa95acdaf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "733a01cd-bc9b-4f62-8bff-b83aa95acdaf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04f74b12-2878-42c9-b6c0-577cef5d5aa1",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define responsiveness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04f74b12-2878-42c9-b6c0-577cef5d5aa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c002160-d1d8-4e02-a750-7a04cd87d663",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test and refine their AI-generated layouts for responsiveness across devices, using checklists and peer review to spot and fix issues.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the key stats aloud to students. Then, read what to consider when creating a responsive, user friendly website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If AI learns from old, non-responsive websites, how might that affect the layouts it creates today?*\n- *How could human designers help guide AI to create more mobile-friendly or flexible layouts?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should think critically about how the data used to train AI can shape the designs it produces. Training a model on older, non-responsive websites could lead AI to create layouts that don't work well on mobile devices. Students should consider how designers can guide or correct AI to make layouts that adapt better to different screen sizes. Some suggestions include: training AI with examples of mobile-friendly websites, using prompts that mention flexible grids or mobile usability, and defining constraints (like minimum button size or readable text) that AI must follow when generating layouts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Software Test Engineer role, also referred to as Quality Assurance (QA) Tester.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"A Day in the Life of a Software Test Engineer at GM\" video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c002160-d1d8-4e02-a750-7a04cd87d663",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11884518-9c87-490f-8b1d-497ad0cf1d7a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll get to practice thinking like a Test Engineer. You'll look at an AI-generated webpage, test it for responsiveness, and then use what you find to guide the AI toward better, more responsive design choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11884518-9c87-490f-8b1d-497ad0cf1d7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce2a9c95-0b2c-4c6d-84e5-2428f67c68a0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Designing for Responsiveness activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Levels 1 through 3. Explain that they will be testing and refining their AI-generated web apps from Lesson 11. If they don't have their webpage from the previous class, instruct them to copy and paste the HTML and CSS files from the exemplar in the instructions. Students open their webpages, resize the browser to simulate different device widths, and log any responsiveness issues using the checklist. They then refine prompts to fix those issues, save versions to track progress, and swap with a partner for peer feedback.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students test and refine, ask them to explain the issues they noticed and how their fixes improve usability across screen sizes. When observing peer review, prompt students to connect their suggestions to common responsive design patterns.",
        "progression_name": "Web App Responsiveness",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, remind them to focus on small, specific fixes (e.g., navigation collapsing, text wrapping, images resizing) rather than trying to solve everything at once."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce2a9c95-0b2c-4c6d-84e5-2428f67c68a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8d1da28-71ae-4b2b-80ec-bc2770d5822d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8d1da28-71ae-4b2b-80ec-bc2770d5822d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0c423cc-eec9-420b-be10-63c12abca060",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why should we design websites that work well on all devices and screen sizes?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that responsive design is more inclusive for all users. Push students to think about who is excluded when websites are not designed responsively, e.g. a website that only displays content well for desktops.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Level 4.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to reflect on their understanding of responsiveness issues by explaining what fixes they found most effective when redesigning their webpage."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This prompt can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of responsiveness in design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0c423cc-eec9-420b-be10-63c12abca060",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e45814fb-b9a3-4811-b4f0-7eaf970ca637",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e45814fb-b9a3-4811-b4f0-7eaf970ca637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "40387f4f-233f-45a7-909e-6b3076890131"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "43fd0162-d311-40a6-a358-55bbeabbcf81",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Level 5 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "43fd0162-d311-40a6-a358-55bbeabbcf81",
        "lesson_activity.key": "40387f4f-233f-45a7-909e-6b3076890131"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77794e7a-fd7c-43ac-8505-5eda2d8a7077",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you could create a magazine about anything, what would it be? Who would read it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect personal interests to an audience or community, priming them to think about purpose and audience in their digital magazine project.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77794e7a-fd7c-43ac-8505-5eda2d8a7077",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01d16791-3307-4052-9640-eced4c4c445e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Digital Magazine Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Digital Magazine Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their magazine concept to showcasing their final product.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01d16791-3307-4052-9640-eced4c4c445e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a1eec47-7bf7-49b9-bf3f-8709ec091076",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a1eec47-7bf7-49b9-bf3f-8709ec091076",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a54e66ff-832d-4d7a-9194-e07a0af3a787",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Define Your Magazine, where students plan their magazine's concept, audience, and design direction. Direct students to open their Digital Magazine Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a54e66ff-832d-4d7a-9194-e07a0af3a787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cdbc385d-6f39-4a76-b595-fec6334cda59",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct student to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Define Your Magazine. Students can use the tools on this level to support their work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"How would you describe your magazine's main audience in one sentence?\"\n- \"What kind of stories or visuals would fit your theme?\"\n- \"How might your design choices make your magazine feel more cohesive?\"\n\nUse these quick check-ins to confirm that students are connecting their creative choices to a clear audience and purpose.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1: Define Your Magazine",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish; they'll refine visuals and layouts later in the project."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cdbc385d-6f39-4a76-b595-fec6334cda59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a85c460f-3fc0-4be6-af6d-d4345c96e3f5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a85c460f-3fc0-4be6-af6d-d4345c96e3f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca791e62-f897-4012-accb-2d1ee4f8547a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Explain that this reflection helps them capture what they learned today and how it will shape their next steps.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close. Highlight that reflection is part of being an intentional designer — it helps them connect each day's work to their larger vision."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca791e62-f897-4012-accb-2d1ee4f8547a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73962de9-f6ee-48ed-8a72-7d59a0c4aee4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73962de9-f6ee-48ed-8a72-7d59a0c4aee4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8255406b-4203-4ef1-b58f-601f0b2e574c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one element you're excited to see come to life from your design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reconnect with their design vision and build enthusiasm to begin the hands-on coding phase. This primes them to think intentionally about how their design ideas translate into real HTML/CSS builds today.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8255406b-4203-4ef1-b58f-601f0b2e574c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82def46b-d238-4186-b410-5944a670a406",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 2 and 3. Direct students to open their Digital Magazine Project Guide and locate the Milestone 2 and 3 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that these milestones move from building to refining. Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first—just to get their layout functioning, then improve it through feedback and iteration."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82def46b-d238-4186-b410-5944a670a406",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0819d1aa-2e99-4cbe-92fa-db74f0dc82ce",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 2 and 3 to work on their first draft of their pages. Clarify that they'll begin building the first draft of their magazine pages using HTML and CSS, with help from the AI tools available at these levels. Encourage students to use AI prompts strategically — for example, to generate layouts, apply color palettes, or organize text elements, while checking that the code makes sense.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- \"What part of your magazine are you starting with?\"\n- \"What feedback from your plan or Milestone 1 are you using as you build?\"\n- \"Have you tested your page to make sure it behaves the way you expect?\"\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck — redirect them to the AI Chat or Project Support Bot for troubleshooting help before intervening directly.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2: Build Your First Draft"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0819d1aa-2e99-4cbe-92fa-db74f0dc82ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc80c524-f720-450d-ab48-3a97167cde0c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3: Refine and Align"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc80c524-f720-450d-ab48-3a97167cde0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e813196c-4355-417b-8b30-f19e7370da7f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights before moving to Milestone 3.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e813196c-4355-417b-8b30-f19e7370da7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57f64b42-21ae-4b5a-89bf-ccdfc2fc58ba",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57f64b42-21ae-4b5a-89bf-ccdfc2fc58ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bca3854-f066-4ea8-a84f-26d0e903d859",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Explain that this reflection helps them identify what's working well and what still needs adjustment before the final milestone. Give students a few minutes to reflect individually, then share with a partner or small group.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that identify concrete next steps (e.g., \"I need to fix spacing,\" \"My navigation isn't consistent,\" or \"I want to make my site more accessible\"). Encourage students to connect these adjustments back to their user goals and audience experience.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one key improvement they plan to make. Highlight that thoughtful reflection is a sign of growth—it shows they're thinking like real designers preparing to polish their final product."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bca3854-f066-4ea8-a84f-26d0e903d859",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff54d47e-d2f2-4365-901d-87a4d19d20b4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What challenge do you still need to solve before your site is ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify one specific challenge or improvement area that will guide their focus during today's Milestone 4 – Test and Finalize work. This primes them to enter work time with clear direction and purpose.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff54d47e-d2f2-4365-901d-87a4d19d20b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "386c8601-1cc0-40e0-ad9d-4317dc5095c9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today is focused on Milestone 4 – Test and Finalize, where students polish their site using feedback from peers, teachers, and their own self-assessment. Remind students to check their Digital Magazine Project Guide for the Milestone 4 deliverables, which include final usability, accessibility, and design checks.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame today as a celebration of their work and growth as designers. Say something like: \"Today's your chance to put the finishing touches on your magazine and share what you've built. Every project is unique — focus on making sure yours reflects your audience, your ideas, and your effort.\" If you're doing a showcase, preview how students will present (e.g., rotating stations, quick pitches, or feedback slips)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "386c8601-1cc0-40e0-ad9d-4317dc5095c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "565bed9c-9f42-4401-8c47-bc02a23965d2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "565bed9c-9f42-4401-8c47-bc02a23965d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9b4cf30-de8c-4ed9-a3cb-be3fc5e942e1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Digital Magazine Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9b4cf30-de8c-4ed9-a3cb-be3fc5e942e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63452eec-ca7f-4bd8-97ea-ae2a5b7b1345",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to finish developing their Digital Magazine projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4 – Test and Finalize. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Digital Magazine project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4: Test and Finalize",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63452eec-ca7f-4bd8-97ea-ae2a5b7b1345",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91651fc1-4ece-4cd8-914a-81e02c6f48f3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91651fc1-4ece-4cd8-914a-81e02c6f48f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e730388-95ef-442f-a39b-868326e7edb6",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their digital magazines on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e730388-95ef-442f-a39b-868326e7edb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "389c1583-feb9-409c-8966-4c63687ce628",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've gone from planning and designing to building, testing, and refining. This reflection is your chance to capture how you've grown — not just as a coder or designer, but as someone who collaborates with AI intentionally.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "389c1583-feb9-409c-8966-4c63687ce628",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e6ad030-d2c4-4bff-9640-04b705809ffa",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Final Reflection and Final Self-Check sections in their Digital Magazine Project Guide. Explain that this final step helps them consolidate their learning and ensure their site meets key design goals — usability, consistency, accessibility, and thoughtful use of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to elaborate on their answers with specific examples of how they used feedback or iteration to improve their project. If students finish early, prompt them to help peers finalize their submissions or review their rubric one more time for completeness.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "You can use the Digital Magazine Planning Guide as evidence for the Decomposition and Design Thinking and rubric criteria. The Final Reflection as evidence for Reflection and Iteration with AI rubric criteria."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e6ad030-d2c4-4bff-9640-04b705809ffa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "edccd685-a867-4e8f-97eb-22c9dcd77ed2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
